const e={intro:["It begins as a movement within Judaism: a Galilean teacher executed under Pontius Pilate, and a circle of Jewish followers in Jerusalem who announce that God has raised him. For perhaps two decades the dividing question is not the nature of Christ but the status of the Torah, and whether a gentile must become a Jew in order to belong. Within a century Paul's answer has become the default, the assemblies of the eastern Mediterranean speak Greek, and a Jewish sect has become a Mediterranean religion with a canon it is still assembling and a rule of faith it is still phrasing.","The fourth century argued about the Son's relation to the Father and produced ὁμοούσιος at Nicaea in 325; the fifth argued about how divinity and humanity are joined in one Christ, and those answers proved untranslatable. The Church of the East kept the Antiochene grammar of two natures and two ܩܢܘܡܐ (qnome); the Oriental Orthodox kept Cyril's μία φύσις; the imperial church kept Leo's ἐν δύο φύσεσιν. Three families walked out of the same argument, each convinced the others had abandoned the Saviour, each carrying away a liturgy, a language and a hierarchy. Modern dialogue has concluded that much of the quarrel was terminology rather than substance — a considerable thing to discover fifteen centuries late.","The later ruptures concern authority and grace rather than natures. Rome and Constantinople drifted through the Filioque, the azymes and the papal claim into the excommunications exchanged by legate and patriarch in 1054, and then into the far more decisive sack of 1204. The Latin West afterwards broke along a seam opening since Wyclif and Hus: how a sinner is justified, who may read scripture in their own tongue, who may say what the church teaches. Luther, Zwingli, Calvin, the Anabaptists and the English crown gave five incompatible answers, and their heirs gave more. Russia tore in 1666 over the sign of the cross; Rome lost the Old Catholics in 1870 over papal infallibility; and every generation since has produced restorers convinced the apostolic church can be recovered by starting again from the text.","The widest published count, in the World Christian Encyclopedia, puts the result at roughly 45,000 denominations. The figure is real and it is routinely abused. Its compilers count an organised Christian body separately in each country where it operates, so a single global communion present in a hundred nations contributes up to a hundred entries, and the Catholic Church alone accounts for hundreds; independent congregations with no wider structure are counted as well. What the number measures is institutional and national differentiation, not 45,000 rival theologies. Set against it are three great communions of the ancient world, four or five confessional families out of the Reformation, and a twentieth-century Pentecostal and independent movement whose scale nobody can measure precisely. The plural in Christianities is not a provocation. It is arithmetic.","A word on the numbers throughout. Almost every membership figure in this atlas is an estimate, and many are contested by the bodies they describe. Churches count baptisms, states count self-identification, and researchers count practice; the three diverge enormously and in predictable political directions. Where a range is given, the range is the honest answer."],groups:[{key:"the-first-communities",image:"7e556e1117a2cf091ddb3500e66a1617.webp",imageAlt:"Elders in prayer shawls seated with scrolls and oil lamps beneath a portico above a Mediterranean harbour, while travellers with packs walk the road down toward the ships",imageCaption:"From Jerusalem to the sea: the assembly of elders still keeping the Law at the table, and the mission walking out past them toward the ships. Both are the same movement in the same decade, and the argument between them is the first argument the church ever had.",label:"The First Communities",blurb:"From a Jewish assembly in Jerusalem to a Greek-speaking movement around the Mediterranean, and the loose coalition of churches that emerged from it with a canon, a rule of faith and a clergy."},{key:"the-parting-arguments",label:"The Parting Arguments",blurb:"The second to the fifth centuries: rival readings of the same gospel, from Marcion's alien God to Arius's created Son to the African church of the martyrs. Most were named heresies by the side that won; each was answered at length because each was serious."},{key:"the-council-churches",image:"ed42c321d22d3f300a5d686660c12d86.webp",imageAlt:"Two processions leaving a great basilica in the rain and walking apart — vested clergy in white and gold down the centre, dark-robed Eastern monks and priests bearing books and censers to the right",imageCaption:"Three roads out of one council. After Chalcedon in 451 the Church of the East, the Oriental Orthodox and the Chalcedonian imperial church walked out of a shared tradition by separate doors — and every communion in this group is still standing on the road it took that day.",label:"The Council Churches",blurb:"The ancient communions, formed and separated by the councils of 431 and 451 and by the long estrangement of Rome and Constantinople. Every one of them is still here."},{key:"medieval-dissent",label:"Medieval Dissent",blurb:"Dualists, apostolic poverty movements and vernacular Bible-readers, from Armenia to the Alps to Bohemia. Nearly all were suppressed; two survived to meet the Reformation, and their descendants are churches still."},{key:"the-reformations",label:"The Reformations",blurb:"Ruptures over authority and grace in the old European churches, from Wittenberg in 1517 to Utrecht in 1889 — and, along a quite different seam, the tearing of Russian Orthodoxy over the sign of the cross."},{key:"the-radical-reformation",image:"6552243505a30743c4d03eb10162dbeb.webp",imageAlt:"A grown man kneeling in a farmhouse kitchen as water is poured over his head from a jug, neighbours standing witness, lantern-carrying soldiers gathering in the rain beyond the window",imageCaption:"Zürich, 1525: the first rebaptism among the Swiss Brethren, performed in a kitchen with the authorities already at the window. The conviction underneath it — that the church is a voluntary body and not an arm of the state — was a capital offence in Catholic and Protestant lands alike.",label:"The Radical Reformation",blurb:"Those who judged the magisterial reformers half-reformed. Believers' baptism, a church wholly separate from the state, community of goods, and at the far edge a denial of the Trinity itself."},{key:"the-english-speaking-explosion",label:"The English-Speaking Explosion",blurb:"From the Elizabethan settlement to the Atlantic revivals: an established church, the dissenters it expelled, and the movements that carried English-language Protestantism across an empire and a continent."},{key:"the-restorationists",label:"The Restorationists",blurb:"Movements convinced that the church of the apostles had been lost and could be built again — from the New Testament text, or from new revelation. American in origin, global in reach, and contested at the doctrinal edge of the family."},{key:"the-global-south",image:"88b3d799e011fde6bdbcfaa43d009c8c.webp",imageAlt:"A large congregation in white robes clapping and singing under a corrugated shelter at dusk, drummers at the front, a crowd with umbrellas spilling into the open air, a city skyline behind",imageCaption:"An African Instituted congregation in Nigeria at dusk: drums at the front, white robes, the crowd spilling into the rain under umbrellas. The centre of gravity of world Christianity moved south within living memory, and this is what it sounds like.",label:"The Pentecostal Century and the Global South",blurb:"The twentieth century's great reordering: a revival on a Los Angeles side street, its spread into every existing church, and the independent Christianities of Africa, China and the Philippines that now hold the movement's centre of gravity."}],entries:[{slug:"jewish-christianity",name:"The Jerusalem Assembly and the Jewish Christians",selfName:"the Way; the Nazoraeans",exonym:"Ebionites — Greek Ebionaioi, from the Hebrew אביונים (ebyonim), 'the poor'",era:"c. 30 – 5th century",group:"the-first-communities",parent:null,origin:"The first community was Jewish, in Jerusalem, and led not by Peter but by James the brother of Jesus, whose execution in 62 is reported by Josephus and by Hegesippus. Its members kept the Torah, attended the Temple while it stood, and understood Jesus as Israel's Messiah rather than the founder of a second religion. After 70 the tradition preserved by Eusebius has the community withdrawing to Pella; historians are divided over whether that flight is memory or apologetic construction. What survived into the second and third centuries were Torah-observant groups east of the Jordan and in Syria, known almost entirely through their opponents — Irenaeus, Origen, Eusebius, Epiphanius. Whether 'the poor' was a name they chose is disputed: Origen derives it from the Hebrew, while Epiphanius invents a founder called Ebion. No text certainly written by them survives.",distinctives:["Continued observance of Torah — circumcision, Sabbath, food laws — as binding on Jewish followers of Jesus, not as an optional inheritance.","The heresiologists report the Ebionites holding Jesus to be the son of Joseph and Mary, chosen at his baptism; the Nazoraeans are described as accepting the virginal conception while remaining Torah-observant.","Hostility to Paul: Epiphanius and the Pseudo-Clementines describe a tradition in which Paul is an apostate from the Law rather than an apostle.","Scholarship is divided on the category itself: an older model of a clean first-century 'parting of the ways' has given way, in Daniel Boyarin and Annette Yoshiko Reed among others, to overlapping Jewish and Christian identities persisting for centuries."],christology:"Reported as adoptionist among the Ebionites — Jesus a righteous man on whom the Spirit descended at the Jordan — and as more conventional among the Nazoraeans. Because every report is hostile and none first-hand, the safest statement is that these communities held Jesus to be Israel's Messiah without holding him a second divine subject alongside the God of Israel, and saw no reason for the Law to lapse.",today:null},{slug:"the-pauline-mission",name:"The Pauline Mission",selfName:"the churches of God in Christ Jesus",exonym:null,era:"c. 33–68",group:"the-first-communities",parent:"The Jerusalem assembly and the Hellenist circle around Stephen",origin:"Paul of Tarsus, a Pharisee who had persecuted the movement, joined it after a visionary encounter he describes as a revelation of God's Son, and spent some twenty years founding small mixed assemblies in Asia Minor, Macedonia, Achaia and finally Rome. Seven of his letters are undisputed and are the earliest Christian writings we possess, all older than any gospel. The decisive meeting, at Jerusalem around 48 or 49 and reported twice with different emphases in Galatians 2 and Acts 15, agreed that gentile converts need not be circumcised. That decision, more than any doctrine, is why there is a world religion rather than a Jewish sect: it made membership portable. He was executed at Rome under Nero, on a tradition first attested late in the first century and elaborated in the second.",distinctives:["Gentiles admitted on faith and baptism without circumcision or the food laws — the ruling that made the movement translatable into any city of the empire.","Justification as God's act rather than the believer's achievement, argued from Abraham and worked out most fully in Galatians and Romans.","A letter-writing pastorate: occasional documents addressed to specific crises which became, within a century, the first Christian canon after the gospels.","Scholars divide sharply over how Jewish Paul remained. The New Perspective of E. P. Sanders and James Dunn reads him as opposing gentile exclusion rather than Jewish 'works-righteousness'; the Paul-within-Judaism school of Mark Nanos and Magnus Zetterholm holds he never left Judaism and legislated only for gentiles."],christology:"High and early. Paul applies to Jesus the language of Israel's God, calls him Lord in texts where the Greek Bible has the divine name, and quotes a hymn describing one who was in the form of God and emptied himself. He does not use the vocabulary of nature and person that later councils would require, and reading his letters as though he did is the commonest anachronism in the subject.",today:"Every church in this atlas that admits gentiles without circumcision is downstream of the Jerusalem agreement, whether or not it reads Paul as Augustine or Luther did."},{slug:"the-great-church",name:"The Great Church",selfName:"the catholic church",exonym:null,era:"c. 150–451",group:"the-first-communities",parent:"The Pauline and other apostolic missions",origin:"By the middle of the second century the pagan critic Celsus could speak of ἡ μεγάλη ἐκκλησία, the great church, meaning the large majority body as distinct from the smaller schools around it. It was never a denomination and had no central government: a network of city churches that recognised one another's bishops, exchanged letters and eucharistic hospitality, and converged on a shared shape. That shape had four pillars — a single bishop in each city with presbyters and deacons; a rule of faith rehearsed at baptism, from which the creeds grew; a fourfold gospel and a growing collection of apostolic letters; and the retention of the Hebrew scriptures as Christian scripture. Irenaeus of Lyon, writing about 180, is its first great theorist, and he argues from succession and public teaching precisely because his opponents argued from secret tradition.",distinctives:["The monarchical episcopate and the argument from apostolic succession: what the apostles taught was taught openly, and can be checked against the churches they founded.","The rule of faith (regula fidei) — a summary of the story from creation to judgement, held before there was a fixed canon and used to interpret it.","Retention of the Old Testament against Marcion, and of a fourfold gospel against both harmonisers and single-gospel churches.","Walter Bauer argued in 1934 that in several regions what later counted as heresy was the earlier and stronger form of Christianity. His thesis reshaped the field; its details have been substantially criticised, and the current consensus is a chastened middle position — diversity was real and early, but a recognisable mainstream was also early."],christology:"Not yet a doctrine but a set of commitments held under strain: that the God of Israel is the Father of Jesus Christ, that Christ is genuinely divine and genuinely fleshly, that he suffered in earnest, and that the Spirit is God's own. The technical vocabulary — οὐσία, ὑπόστασις, φύσις — was not yet stable, which is exactly why the fourth and fifth centuries were spent fixing it.",today:null},{slug:"marcionites",name:"The Marcionite Church",selfName:"the church of the good and alien God",exonym:"Marcionites",era:"c. 144 – 10th century",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"The Pauline mission, read without the Old Testament",origin:"Marcion of Sinope, a Pontic shipowner of some wealth, came to Rome and was expelled from the church there in a year traditionally reckoned as 144. He founded not a school but a church, with bishops, a liturgy, a baptism and a canon, planted across the empire and vigorous enough that Justin Martyr could report about a decade later that Marcion had taught men of every nation. His starting point was Paul's contrast of law and gospel taken to its limit: the God who made this world, gave the Law and judged Israel is just but not good, and is not the Father whom Jesus revealed; that Father was previously unknown, and came in Christ to ransom souls that were never his own. Marcionite congregations survived in Syria into the fifth century, and are reported far later in the east.",distinctives:["Two gods: the just Creator of the Hebrew scriptures and the good alien God revealed only in Christ. Marcion's lost Antitheses set the two side by side, verse against verse.","The first fixed Christian canon: a single edited gospel close to Luke, and ten letters of Paul, with the Old Testament wholly excluded.","Severe asceticism — celibacy required of the baptised, abstinence from meat and wine — as a refusal to propagate the Creator's world.","Marcion's place in canon history is contested. Adolf von Harnack held that his canon forced the church to make its own; Judith Lieu, Jason BeDuhn and Markus Vinzent have reopened the question whether his gospel was an abridgement of Luke or a version standing behind it. The majority still holds it an abridgement.","Extinct as an organised body; the polemics of Tertullian, Irenaeus and Ephrem the Syrian are its chief memorial."],christology:"A pure docetism in the service of a moral argument. Christ is the manifestation of the good God in human seeming, unbegotten, unborn and unentangled with matter, appearing in the fifteenth year of Tiberius without birth or growth; the crucifixion is real as an event and unreal as a suffering of flesh. Opponents replied that a Saviour who did not take our flesh cannot have saved it — the argument Irenaeus and later Athanasius made the hinge of orthodoxy.",today:null},{slug:"valentinians",name:"The Valentinian School",selfName:"Christians — they used no separate name; 'the spiritual' (πνευματικοί) is their own term for the elect",exonym:"Gnostics (γνωστικοί); Valentinians",era:"c. 140 – 4th century",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"Alexandrian Christianity and the Pauline letters read allegorically",origin:"Valentinus, an Alexandrian who taught at Rome from about 136, was the most sophisticated Christian intellectual of his generation and, on Tertullian's hostile report, a disappointed candidate for its bishopric. His pupils — Ptolemy, Heracleon, Theodotus, Marcus — formed a school rather than a rival church, and generally attended the ordinary congregations while holding an inner reading of their sacraments. Their myth tells of a divine fullness, the πλήρωμα, of paired aeons; the fall of the youngest, Sophia; the making of the material world by a Demiurge who does not know the Father; and the recovery of the fallen spiritual element through the Saviour's coming and the giving of knowledge. Until 1945 the school was known almost entirely from its enemies. The Nag Hammadi codices gave back its own voice.",distinctives:["Salvation by γνῶσις — knowledge not as information but as recognition of one's origin, awakening the divine spark to its source.","Heracleon wrote the first known commentary on the Gospel of John; the school's allegorical method was a serious rival exegesis, not a rejection of scripture.","The three classes of humanity — spiritual, soulish, material — were long read as rigid determinism; Einar Thomassen and Ismo Dunderberg argue the categories were more fluid and paraenetic than the heresiologists allowed.","'Gnosticism' as a unified religion is itself under challenge: Michael Williams and Karen King have argued the category is a modern construct assembled out of ancient polemic, and many specialists now prefer to speak of specific schools."],christology:"Layered rather than simple. The Saviour who descends is a being of the fullness; the Jesus who is seen is variously described as a psychic Christ, or as a body prepared for the descent, with the divine element departing before the passion in some accounts. Irenaeus objected that this severs the Redeemer from the redeemed and makes the incarnation a costume; the Valentinian reply, visible in the Gospel of Truth, is that the crucifixion is precisely where the Father's unknown name is published.",today:null},{slug:"arians-and-homoians",name:"Arius and the Homoian Churches",selfName:"Christians, confessing the Son like the Father according to the Scriptures",exonym:"Arians — a label applied by their opponents to a range of positions, most of which did not derive from Arius",era:"c. 318 – 7th century",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"The Alexandrian and Antiochene debate over the Son's relation to the Father",origin:"About 318 Arius, a presbyter of the Baucalis church in Alexandria, fell out with his bishop Alexander over how the Son is related to the Father. Constantine summoned the Council of Nicaea in 325, which confessed the Son ὁμοούσιος — of one substance — with the Father and anathematised the formula ἦν ποτε ὅτε οὐκ ἦν, 'there was when he was not'. That did not settle it. For sixty years emperors, synods and exiles moved the question back and forth, and after the councils of Ariminum and Seleucia in 359 and Constantinople in 360 the empire's official confession was Homoian. Jerome's line that the world groaned to find itself Arian belongs to that moment. Constantinople in 381 reaffirmed Nicaea, and the Homoian confession survived outside the empire among the Germanic peoples until the seventh century.",distinctives:["What Arius actually taught, from his three surviving documents: the Son is God's own perfect creature, begotten before all ages and before time, the one through whom everything else was made, but not co-eternal and not from the Father's substance. He is called God by grace and participation, not by nature.","His motive was monotheism. Only the Father is ἀγέννητος, unbegotten and without origin; to make the Son of the Father's substance seemed to Arius either to divide God materially or to collapse Father and Son into one, the error of Sabellius.","The sources are hostile. Only two letters and a confession survive from Arius himself; his Thalia is known through Athanasius, who quotes it in two mutually inconsistent forms while refuting it.","Four positions, not two: Nicenes (ὁμοούσιος), Homoiousians around Basil of Ancyra (ὁμοιούσιος, like in substance), Homoians (ὅμοιος, like, without specifying substance), and the Heteroousians of Aetius and Eunomius (ἀνόμοιος, unlike).","The Germanic churches: Ulfilas translated the Bible into Gothic as a Homoian, and Goths, Vandals, Burgundians and Lombards held that confession for centuries. Visigothic Spain conformed to Nicaea at the Third Council of Toledo in 589; the Lombards late in the seventh century.","'Arianism' as a single coherent party is now widely doubted. Rowan Williams, R. P. C. Hanson, Maurice Wiles and Lewis Ayres have argued that Athanasius assembled the label out of polemic, and that the fourth century was a search for a doctrine of God rather than the defence of one already settled. Modern non-trinitarian bodies to whom the label is applied likewise reject the genealogy, and none cites Arius as an authority."],christology:"The Son is the first and greatest of all that is, God's Word and Wisdom, agent of creation and object of Christian worship — and yet a creature, with a beginning, distinct in substance from the Father. The Nicene objection was soteriological before it was metaphysical: if the Son is a creature then a creature is being worshipped, and a creature cannot deify what it has not got to give. Athanasius put the whole weight there — only God can make us God's.",today:null},{slug:"montanists",name:"Montanism",selfName:"the New Prophecy (ἡ νέα προφητεία)",exonym:"the Phrygian heresy; Cataphrygians",era:"c. 165 – 6th century",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"The prophetic life of the Asian churches",origin:"In a Phrygian village a convert named Montanus began to prophesy in ecstasy, speaking in the first person as the Paraclete; two women, Prisca and Maximilla, prophesied with him and were reckoned his equals. The date is uncertain — Epiphanius implies about 157, Eusebius about 172 — and 165 is a conventional middle. The movement called itself simply the New Prophecy. It was not a doctrinal revolt: its creed was that of the wider church, and this is the point most often missed. What it claimed was that revelation had not ceased and that the church had grown soft. Pepuza and Tymion in Phrygia were designated as the place where the New Jerusalem would descend; Tymion was located by inscription only in 2000–2001. Asian synods condemned it, and imperial legislation under Justinian finished it in the sixth century.",distinctives:["Continuing prophecy, uttered in ecstasy and collected as oracles, set alongside rather than under the apostolic writings — the precise claim the emerging canon was designed to close.","Women in authority: Epiphanius reports Montanist women serving as bishops and presbyters, and Prisca and Maximilla are named prophets, not adjuncts.","Rigorism — extended fasts (the xerophagiae), a ban on second marriages, refusal of flight under persecution, a restricted view of post-baptismal forgiveness.","Tertullian of Carthage, the most formidable Latin theologian of his day, embraced the New Prophecy from about 207. Whether he ever formally left the catholic church, or led a separate body, is disputed among historians.","Archaeology at Pepuza and Tymion, led by William Tabbernee and Peter Lampe, has recovered the movement's geography if not its people."],christology:"Conventional for its time and place. The controversy turned on pneumatology and on office: whether the παράκλητος promised in John continued to speak in new mouths, and whether an ecstatic prophet outranked a bishop. Opponents such as the Anonymous quoted by Eusebius argued that true prophecy is not ecstatic possession; the Montanists answered that the church had domesticated the Spirit.",today:null},{slug:"manichaeans",name:"Manichaeism",selfName:"the Religion of Light",exonym:"Manichaeans",era:"c. 240 – 17th century",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"Elchasaite Jewish-Christian baptists of Mesopotamia",origin:"This entry carries a flag: Manichaeism is a distinct world religion, not a Christian denomination. It is here because it presented itself as the completion of Christianity and because the churches of the Roman world spent three centuries arguing with it. Mani, born in Mesopotamia in 216 and raised in an Elchasaite Jewish-Christian baptist community, received revelations from a heavenly Twin, called himself an apostle of Jesus Christ, and wrote his own scriptures rather than leaving them to disciples — a deliberate correction, as he saw it, of the failures of Jesus, Zoroaster and the Buddha, all of whom he honoured as prior messengers. He was executed under the Sasanian king Bahram I about 274 to 277. His religion ran from Roman Africa to the Tarim Basin.",distinctives:["Two eternal principles, Light and Darkness, and a cosmic history in which particles of light are trapped in matter and gradually liberated — evil as a substance, not a privation.","A canon written by the founder, including the Living Gospel and the Shabuhragan; recovered in the twentieth century through the Coptic Kephalaia from Medinet Madi and the Greek Cologne Mani Codex.","A two-tier community: the Elect, bound to celibacy and a strict diet that released light through their bodies, and the Hearers who supported them.","Deliberate translatability — Mani's missionaries recast the system in Christian terms in the west, Buddhist terms in the east, Zoroastrian terms in Iran, which is why its Christian face was convincing enough to alarm bishops. The Uyghur Khaganate adopted it as a state religion in 762; Augustine was a Hearer for nine years before his conversion.","Extinct as a living faith. Its last community persisted in Fujian; the Cao'an near Jinjiang, with a carved Mani figure dated 1339, survives as the only Manichaean temple building in the world, now maintained as a Buddhist site.","Augustine's anti-Manichaean writings shaped western thinking on evil, free will and the goodness of creation for a thousand years — the influence of an opponent is part of the story."],christology:"Three figures bear the name Jesus: the Jesus of Light, a redeemer who awakens Adam; the suffering Jesus (Jesus patibilis) diffused in the world's matter as trapped light; and the historical Jesus, a messenger whose crucifixion was in appearance only, since a being of Light cannot suffer in flesh. Christian opponents objected on all three counts — the denial of a real incarnation, of a good creation, and that the God of the Old Testament is the Father of Christ.",today:null},{slug:"donatists",name:"The Donatist Church",selfName:"the catholic church of the martyrs",exonym:"Donatists",era:"311 – c. 700",group:"the-parting-arguments",parent:"The African church of Tertullian and Cyprian",origin:"The Diocletianic persecution had ceased in Africa by 305 and left a question: what to do about the traditores, clergy who had handed over the scriptures to be burned. In 311 Caecilian was made bishop of Carthage by a consecrator alleged to have been one of them, and the Numidian bishops elected a rival, succeeded from 313 by Donatus, whose long and able leadership gave the movement its name. Rome and the Council of Arles in 314 found against them, and Constantine backed the judgement with force — the first time an emperor coerced Christians on behalf of other Christians. The Donatists were not a fringe: in Numidia they were the majority church for a century, with their own councils, martyrs and vast basilicas. A state-run conference at Carthage in 411 ruled against them; they persisted, and Gregory the Great was still writing about them at the end of the sixth century.",distinctives:["The holiness of the church understood as the actual purity of its ministers: sacraments conferred by a traditor are void, and those baptised by one must be baptised again.","A self-understanding as the church of the martyrs and true heir of Cyprian, against a Mediterranean communion that had, as they saw it, made its peace with the persecutor.","Augustine's counter-arguments became western doctrine: the church is a mixed body of wheat and tares until the harvest, and a sacrament's validity rests on Christ, not the minister's virtue.","Augustine also came, reluctantly, to justify imperial coercion of schismatics from the parable's 'compel them to come in' — a text with a long and terrible afterlife, and one his admirers do not defend.","The Circumcellions, bands associated with the movement, were long read as an agrarian social revolt; Brent Shaw's Sacred Violence has substantially revised that older account of W. H. C. Frend in favour of a religious reading.","Extinct with the collapse of Latin Christianity in North Africa after the Arab conquest of the late seventh century."],christology:"Indistinguishable from that of their opponents; both sides were Nicene, used the same liturgy and read the same Latin Bible. The Donatist schism is the purest case in the family of a division about the church rather than about Christ — which is why it recurs, in different costume, in every later movement that asks whether a compromised institution can still be the body of Christ.",today:null},{slug:"church-of-the-east",name:"The Church of the East",selfName:"Assyrian Church of the East",exonym:"Nestorian",era:"2nd century – present; its own tradition traces to the apostles Thomas, Addai and Mari",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Syriac-speaking churches of Edessa and Mesopotamia",origin:"This is the church that grew up outside the Roman empire. Syriac-speaking Christianity took root at Edessa and along the Tigris in the second century, and under the Sasanian shahs it organised itself around the see of Seleucia-Ctesiphon: the Synod of Isaac in 410 gave it a structure, and the Synod of Dadisho in 424 declared that its catholicos would answer to no bishop of the western, that is Roman, empire. That independence was political before it was doctrinal, and it was a matter of survival — a church suspected of loyalty to Rome was a church persecuted in Persia. Theologically it inherited the Antiochene tradition of Theodore of Mopsuestia, its great exegete, and it did not receive the Council of Ephesus of 431. From the seventh century it ran the most extensive mission network in Christian history, from Malabar to Chang'an.",distinctives:["It rejects the label 'Nestorian' and has done so formally and repeatedly. It does not derive from Nestorius, was not founded by him, and received its Christology from Theodore of Mopsuestia; it honours Nestorius among the Greek teachers as a man unjustly condemned, which is not the same as taking him for a founder. The name is a Roman polemical exonym, and this portal uses it only to identify what others have meant.","Its grammar of the incarnation: two natures and two ܩܢܘܡܐ (qnome, concrete individual subsistences) in one ܦܪܨܘܦܐ (parsopa) of filiation. Qnoma has no exact Greek or English equivalent, and a great deal of the fifteen-hundred-year quarrel is a failure of translation between it and ὑπόστασις.","The Xi'an stele, 大秦景教流行中國碑, erected in 781 and unearthed in the 1620s, records the arrival of the monk Alopen at the Tang capital in 635 — the earliest documentary evidence of Christianity in China.","A metropolitan province in India from at least the sixth century, giving the St Thomas Christians their East Syriac liturgy and hierarchy until the Portuguese arrived.","The 1994 Common Christological Declaration signed by Pope John Paul II and Patriarch Mar Dinkha IV affirmed a shared confession of Christ and accepted that the differing formulas about Mary — Mother of Christ and Mother of God — are legitimate in their respective traditions. It was the formal end of the Nestorian charge between those two churches.","Modern catastrophe: the Ottoman-era massacres and the Sayfo of 1915, the Simele massacre in Iraq in 1933, the flight from the Nineveh Plain after 2014, and a patriarchate now seated at Erbil."],christology:"Two natures, each with its own qnoma, united in one prosopon — the one Christ, Son of God, in whom the divinity did not suffer and the humanity was not absorbed. It refuses the term θεοτόκος standing alone, not to demean Mary but to protect the distinction, and prefers 'Mother of Christ our God and Saviour'. Chalcedonian and Oriental Orthodox critics have read this as two sons; the church answers that the union in one prosopon is precisely what forbids two sons, and that its liturgy has confessed one Lord Jesus Christ throughout.",today:"A small church with a very large history. Estimates run from a few hundred thousand to around half a million, most now in diaspora in the United States, Australia, Sweden and the Gulf; no reliable census exists and circulated figures vary by a factor of two. The Ancient Church of the East separated in 1968 over the calendar and the hereditary patriarchate, with reunion talks proceeding intermittently since 2010. The Chaldean Catholic Church, in communion with Rome after a schism of 1552, is now much the larger body, and the Syro-Malabar Church of India shares the same East Syriac inheritance."},{slug:"oriental-orthodox",name:"The Oriental Orthodox Communion",selfName:"the Orthodox Church; the miaphysite churches",exonym:"Monophysite — a charge these churches reject and anathematise",era:"451 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Alexandrian tradition of Athanasius and Cyril",origin:"In 451 the Council of Chalcedon defined that Christ is acknowledged in two natures. A large part of Egypt, Syria, Armenia and, in time, Ethiopia and India refused the formula, holding that it divided the one Christ and betrayed Cyril of Alexandria, whose language they had been given as the test of orthodoxy only twenty years earlier at Ephesus. Two years before Chalcedon, a council at Ephesus in 449 presided over by Dioscorus of Alexandria had vindicated the miaphysite side; Pope Leo called it a latrocinium, a den of robbers, and the name Robber Council has stuck in Western textbooks ever since. It is Leo's polemic, not a neutral description, and the churches that assembled there have never used it. The refusal was at first a party within one church, and emperors tried for eighty years to bridge it — the Henotikon of 482 produced instead a thirty-five-year schism with Rome. Under Justinian the breach became structural.",distinctives:["Miaphysite, not monophysite, and the distinction is not pedantry — it is the difference between what these churches confess and what they anathematise. They confess μία φύσις τοῦ Θεοῦ Λόγου σεσαρκωμένη, one incarnate nature of God the Word, in which divinity and humanity are united without mixture, change, confusion or separation, each retaining all its properties. They condemn Eutyches, who taught that the humanity was absorbed like a drop of water in the sea, as firmly as Chalcedon did. To call them monophysite is to attribute to them a heresy they have excommunicated people for holding.","The formula μία φύσις came to Cyril through writings he believed to be Athanasius's and which are now known to be Apollinarian in origin — a fact Chalcedonians and miaphysites read in opposite directions, and one no honest account should omit.","Severus of Antioch (c. 465–538) is the communion's principal systematic theologian; his correspondence, largely surviving only in Syriac, is the classic exposition.","Six churches in full communion: the Coptic Orthodox, Syriac Orthodox, Armenian Apostolic, Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo, Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo and Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church of India. There is no primatial see over them.","The joint commission of Chalcedonian and Oriental Orthodox theologians concluded, at the Anba Bishoy monastery in 1989 and at Chambésy in 1990, that the two families hold the same faith in Christ in different terminologies, and recommended lifting the mutual anathemas. Bilateral declarations with Rome — Paul VI and Shenouda III in 1973, John Paul II and Ignatius Zakka I in 1984 — said the same. Formal reunion has nonetheless not followed, chiefly over the status of the councils and the anathematised saints on both sides."],christology:"One incarnate nature of the Word, composite and undivided: the humanity is complete, consubstantial with ours, and never for a moment ceases to be humanity, but it has no independent subsistence apart from the Word who assumed it. The objection from Chalcedon is that a single nature cannot preserve two full sets of properties; the miaphysite answer is that φύσις here means the one concrete existing Christ, not an abstract essence, and that 'in two natures' invites exactly the division Nestorius was condemned for.",today:"Estimates vary very widely and no common count exists. The churches' own claims total somewhere above sixty million, the great majority Ethiopian, with the rest concentrated in Egypt, Armenia, Syria, Eritrea and Kerala and substantial diasporas in Europe, North America and Australia; external estimates run lower. Several of these churches have spent the last century under conditions of genocide, revolution or war, and their demography reflects it."},{slug:"coptic-orthodox",name:"The Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria",selfName:"the See of St Mark",exonym:null,era:"1st century – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Oriental Orthodox communion",origin:"Tradition makes Mark the Evangelist the founder of the church at Alexandria in the 40s, and martyr there. What is certain is that by the late second century Alexandria had the most formidable school in Christendom — Pantaenus, Clement, Origen — and that in the fourth it produced Athanasius, who fought the Arian controversy through five exiles, and in the fifth Cyril, whose Christology the whole eastern church was required to sign. It also invented monasticism: Antony in the desert from about 270, Pachomius organising the first communities on the Nile around 320. When Dioscorus of Alexandria — who had presided two years earlier over the council of 449 that Leo afterwards branded a latrocinium — was deposed at Chalcedon in 451, Egypt did not follow the council. A parallel patriarchate was established, and Egyptian Christianity has been Coptic Orthodox ever since.",distinctives:["The Coptic language, the last stage of ancient Egyptian written in a Greek-derived alphabet, retained in the liturgy (Bohairic dialect) though Arabic is now the language of preaching.","The Calendar of the Martyrs, Anno Martyrum, counting years from 284, the accession of Diocletian — a church that dates its era from a persecution rather than from an incarnation or a hijra.","The birthplace of monasticism: the Desert Fathers, the Sayings, and the monasteries of Wadi El Natrun and the Red Sea, from which the Coptic Pope has been drawn.","A twentieth-century revival largely lay and educational in character — the Sunday School movement of the 1930s and 1940s, out of which came Pope Shenouda III and the current Pope Tawadros II, enthroned in 2012.","A Coptic Catholic Church, in communion with Rome, exists alongside it, as does a small Coptic Evangelical (Presbyterian) Church."],christology:"Cyrilline and miaphysite: one incarnate nature of God the Word, with the humanity entire and unabsorbed. Egypt's refusal of Chalcedon was not a rejection of Christ's humanity but a conviction that Leo's Tome, by speaking of each nature doing what is proper to it, spoke of two acting subjects. The Coptic Church anathematises Eutyches and has said so in every modern dialogue it has entered.",today:"The largest Christian community in the Middle East. Estimates of its size in Egypt run from about six million to fifteen million, and the width of that range is itself a political fact: government figures sit at the low end, church figures at the high. A large diaspora exists in North America, Australia and Europe. The community has endured serious violence in the past two decades — church bombings, the Maspero killings of 2011 — alongside periods of state protection, and emigration has been heavy."},{slug:"armenian-apostolic",name:"The Armenian Apostolic Church",selfName:"Հայ Առաքելական Եկեղեցի (Hay Aṙakelakan Yekeghetsi)",exonym:"Gregorian",era:"301 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Oriental Orthodox communion",origin:"Armenia claims to be the first state to adopt Christianity, when Gregory the Illuminator converted King Trdat III (numbered IV in some scholarship) — traditionally in 301, though many historians prefer a date around 314, after Constantine's edict. Apostolic foundation is credited to Thaddeus and Bartholomew. The decisive event for Armenian identity came a century later: about 405 Mesrop Mashtots devised an alphabet for the language, and the Bible translated into it became the foundation of Armenian literature, a translation of such quality that Armenians call it the Queen of Translations. Armenia's bishops were not at Chalcedon in 451 — that was the year of the Battle of Avarayr against Sasanian Persia, fought over the demand that Armenia return to Zoroastrianism — and when the church came to consider the council at the assemblies of Dvin in 506 and 555, it declined it.",distinctives:["The Armenian alphabet, created for scripture, and a liturgical language (Grabar) that has carried a national identity through five centuries without a state.","Two catholicosates in communion: Etchmiadzin, the Mother See and seat of the Catholicos of All Armenians, and the Catholicosate of the Great House of Cilicia at Antelias in Lebanon, together with the patriarchates of Jerusalem and Constantinople — a structure shaped by the dispersals of the twentieth century.","Distinctive practice: unleavened bread and undiluted wine at the eucharist, Christmas and Theophany kept together on 6 January, and the matagh, an offering of a sacrificed animal shared with the poor, which the church distinguishes carefully from sacrifice.","The khachkar, the carved cross-stone, and a tradition of illumination and architecture — Zvartnots, Geghard, Ani — among the ancient world's great artistic inheritances.","The genocide of 1915, in which the church lost the majority of its historic dioceses along with much of the Armenian population of Anatolia, and which shaped the modern diaspora that now holds most Armenians.","An Armenian Catholic Church, formed in 1742, and Armenian Evangelical churches from the nineteenth century exist alongside the Apostolic Church."],christology:"Miaphysite, in communion with the Copts, Syriacs, Ethiopians, Eritreans and Malankara Orthodox: one united nature of the incarnate Word, the humanity complete and unconfused. Armenian theologians have often stressed that their objection to Chalcedon was as much to the manner of its imposition, and to the circumstances of a church fighting for survival in the east, as to its formula. The joint declaration signed by Karekin I and John Paul II in 1996 affirmed a common faith in the mystery of the Word incarnate.",today:"The national church of Armenia and of a worldwide diaspora, with something in the order of six to nine million adherents claimed, against a resident population in Armenia itself of under three million. The Catholicos of All Armenians has been Karekin II since 1999. The 2020 war over Nagorno-Karabakh and the exodus of 2023 cost the church its communities and monasteries in Artsakh, including sites of the ninth and tenth centuries."},{slug:"ethiopian-tewahedo",name:"The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church",selfName:"ተዋሕዶ (Täwaḥədo) — 'made one'",exonym:null,era:"c. 330 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria",origin:"About 330 a shipwrecked Syrian youth named Frumentius became tutor at the Aksumite court, converted King Ezana, and was consecrated first bishop of Aksum by Athanasius of Alexandria — which is why Ethiopia's metropolitan was appointed from Egypt for the next sixteen centuries. Ezana's coins, changing from crescent-and-disc to cross, are the physical evidence. In the late fifth century the Nine Saints, monks of Syrian background, brought monasticism and translated scripture into Ge'ez. Isolated by the Islamic conquests from the seventh century, the Ethiopian church developed one of the most distinctive Christian cultures in the world: the rock-hewn churches of Lalibela, a vast Ge'ez literature, the Kebra Nagast, and a liturgical life of drums, sistra and the dance of the debtera.",distinctives:["The broadest biblical canon in Christendom — reckoned at eighty-one books — and the only church to canonise 1 Enoch and Jubilees, both of which survive complete only in Ge'ez.","Practices shared with Judaism and much debated by scholars: observance of both Saturday and Sunday, circumcision on the eighth day, dietary restrictions, and the tabot, a consecrated altar tablet representing the Ark, without which no church is a church.","The claim, made at Our Lady Mary of Zion in Aksum, to hold the Ark of the Covenant, guarded by a single monk who alone may see it. The church regards it as fact; historians regard the tradition as documented from the Kebra Nagast of the fourteenth century.","The rock-hewn churches of Lalibela, cut downward out of living stone in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries as a new Jerusalem, and still parish churches rather than monuments.","Autocephaly in stages: an Ethiopian metropolitan for the first time in 1951, a patriarchate in 1959 under Abune Basilios, with the Coptic Pope retaining honorary primacy. The Eritrean Orthodox Tewahedo Church became separately autocephalous in the 1990s after Eritrean independence.","Twentieth-century upheaval: the Italian occupation and the murder of Abune Petros in 1936; the Derg's deposition and killing of Patriarch Theophilos in the 1970s; and continuing internal disputes over ethnic representation in the hierarchy, sharply so since 2018."],christology:"Tewahedo means 'being made one', and the church's name is its doctrine: the union of divinity and humanity in Christ is one nature, one person, without mixture, confusion, division or alteration. Ethiopian theology developed its own controversies within that frame, most famously the seventeenth- and eighteenth-century disputes over the anointing (Qibat, Tewahedo and Ṣägga parties) which set monastic houses against one another for two centuries and were settled politically at the Council of Boru Meda in 1878.",today:"The largest of the Oriental Orthodox churches by a wide margin and one of the largest single churches on earth, with estimates commonly ranging from about 36 million to over 50 million; Ethiopia's 2007 census returned 43.5 per cent of the population as Orthodox, and the population has grown greatly since. Patriarch Abune Mathias has held office since 2013. The Ethiopian Catholic Church and a large evangelical Protestant community (the P'ent'ay) exist alongside it."},{slug:"syriac-orthodox",name:"The Syriac Orthodox Church of Antioch",selfName:"the Syriac Orthodox Church of Antioch and All the East; its people call themselves ܣܘܪܝܝܐ (Suryoye)",exonym:"Jacobite; Monophysite — the church uses neither of itself and rejects the second as a heresy it condemns",era:"1st century – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Oriental Orthodox communion",origin:"Antioch is where the disciples were first called Christians, and the Syriac Orthodox Church holds the apostolic see of Antioch in its own line. Its language is Syriac, the Aramaic dialect of Edessa, and its scriptures the Peshitta; its literature — Ephrem the Syrian's hymns, Jacob of Serugh's verse homilies, Bar Hebraeus's encyclopaedic learning — is one of the three great patristic corpora alongside Greek and Latin, and the least read in the West. After Chalcedon the Syrian miaphysites were leaderless until Jacob Baradaeus, consecrated in 543, spent more than three decades travelling in disguise ordaining bishops and priests; his work created the parallel hierarchy that survives, and gave opponents the exonym Jacobite.",distinctives:["The West Syriac (Serto) script and the liturgical tradition of St James, still sung in the language of Jesus' own Aramaic family, though Arabic, Turoyo, Malayalam and Swedish are now also used.","Tur Abdin, the limestone plateau in south-eastern Turkey, the church's monastic heartland; Mor Gabriel, founded in 397, is among the oldest working monasteries in the world and has spent the last two decades in Turkish land litigation.","Severus of Antioch, patriarch from 512 to 518 and the communion's foremost theologian, whose works survive largely in Syriac translation after the Greek originals were destroyed.","The Sayfo of 1915 — 'the sword' — in which the Syriac Orthodox, along with Assyrians and Chaldeans, were massacred and deported alongside the Armenians; several states have recognised it as genocide and Turkey does not.","A patriarchate moved to Homs in 1933 and Damascus in 1959, and a church now more diasporic than resident: Sweden, Germany, the Netherlands, the United States and Australia hold large communities, and the Syrian war has emptied much of the homeland.","The Jacobite Syrian Christian Church of Malankara in Kerala is part of this church, under the Patriarch of Antioch, and has been in prolonged litigation with the autocephalous Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church."],christology:"Miaphysite, in the tradition of Cyril and Severus: one incarnate nature of the Word from two, the humanity complete and consubstantial with ours. The common declaration signed in 1984 by Pope John Paul II and Patriarch Ignatius Zakka I Iwas stated that the divisions of the fifth century arose from differences of terminology and culture, and permitted the faithful of each church to receive sacraments from the other's clergy in case of need — one of the most concrete ecumenical results anywhere in this atlas.",today:"Perhaps 1.5 to 2 million faithful worldwide including the Indian dioceses, with Patriarch Ignatius Aphrem II in office since 2014. Two bishops of Aleppo, one Syriac Orthodox and one Greek Orthodox, were abducted in 2013 and have not been seen since. A Syriac Catholic Church, in communion with Rome since the eighteenth century, exists alongside it."},{slug:"malankara",name:"The St Thomas Christians of Malankara",selfName:"Nasrani; Mar Thoma Christians",exonym:null,era:"traditionally 52 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Church of the East, and after 1653 the Syriac Orthodox tradition",origin:"The Christians of the Malabar coast hold that the apostle Thomas landed at Muziris in 52 and founded seven churches. Historians can confirm no such thing and can confirm a great deal else: a settled Syriac-speaking Christian community in Kerala, in communion with the Church of the East and receiving bishops from Mesopotamia, is attested from at least the sixth century, embedded in Hindu Kerala with a recognised caste standing and trading privileges recorded on copper plates. The Portuguese arrived in 1498, found this church, and set about latinising it: the Synod of Diamper in 1599 burned Syriac books and imposed Roman usage. On 3 January 1653, at Mattancherry, a crowd of Malabar Christians grasped ropes tied to a stone cross and swore they would no longer obey the Portuguese archbishop — the Coonan Cross Oath. The community has been divided ever since.",distinctives:["One apostolic community, at least seven present-day churches: the Syro-Malabar Catholic Church (East Syriac, in communion with Rome, the second-largest Eastern Catholic church in the world); the Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church (autocephalous, with its own Catholicos at Kottayam); the Jacobite Syrian Christian Church (under the Syriac Orthodox Patriarch of Antioch); the Mar Thoma Syrian Church; the Syro-Malankara Catholic Church (from 1930, under Mar Ivanios); the St Thomas Evangelical Church; and the Chaldean Syrian Church of Thrissur under the Assyrian Church of the East.","Two liturgical inheritances in one community: East Syriac for those who kept the older Mesopotamian rite, West Syriac for those who took the Antiochene rite after 1653.","A church indigenous to India for far longer than it has been touched by Europe, with Kerala's own social forms and a self-understanding as high-caste Indian rather than as a colonial import.","The Malankara Orthodox and Jacobite dispute over the parish churches has run through Indian courts for a century; the Supreme Court of India upheld the 1934 constitution of the Malankara Church in 2017, and the dispute has nevertheless continued on the ground.","The Mar Thoma Syrian Church, shaped by nineteenth-century contact with the Church Missionary Society, keeps an Oriental liturgy with reformed theology and is in full communion with the Anglican Communion — a genuinely unusual combination."],christology:"It depends which branch: East Syriac dyophysite among those in the Church of the East tradition, miaphysite among the Orthodox and Jacobite churches, Chalcedonian among those in communion with Rome or Canterbury. The community's shared conviction long predates all of these formulas — that the faith reached India from an apostle, and not from Europe.",today:"Several million St Thomas Christians in all, most in Kerala and with substantial migration to the Gulf, North America and Europe: the Syro-Malabar Church alone reports over four million. The precise apportionment among the branches is contested by the branches themselves."},{slug:"chalcedonian-imperial-church",name:"The Chalcedonian Imperial Church",selfName:"the one holy catholic and apostolic church",exonym:"Melkite (from Syriac malkā, 'king' — the emperor's church)",era:"451–1054",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Great Church",origin:"The church of the councils and of the Roman state, running from Chalcedon in 451 to the estrangement of its Latin and Greek halves six centuries later. Chalcedon defined one Christ acknowledged in two natures, unconfused, unchangeable, indivisible, inseparable (ἀσυγχύτως, ἀτρέπτως, ἀδιαιρέτως, ἀχωρίστως), concurring in one person and one hypostasis — and immediately lost Egypt, much of Syria and Armenia by doing so. Its subsequent history is the working-out of the consequences: Justinian's condemnation of the Three Chapters at Constantinople II in 553, the failed monothelite compromise settled against itself at Constantinople III in 681, the iconoclast crisis resolved at Nicaea II in 787 and again in 843. It governed itself as a pentarchy of five patriarchal sees, three of which passed under Islamic rule in the seventh century.",distinctives:["The Chalcedonian definition, and with it the technical settlement that φύσις and ὑπόστασις are not synonyms: two natures, one hypostasis.","Canon 28 of Chalcedon, granting Constantinople privileges equal to Rome's because it was the imperial city — rejected by Pope Leo, and the first clear statement of the two theories of primacy that would eventually divide the church.","A settled relation of church and empire: emperors convoked councils, ratified canons, appointed and deposed patriarchs. The Byzantine term for the ideal was symphonia; its critics have other names for it.","Its missions carried the faith beyond the empire — Germanic peoples through Ulfilas's Gothic Bible in a Homoian form later suppressed, the Slavs through Cyril and Methodius from 863, and the Rus' from 988.","It did not survive its own success: no single event ended it, but by the eleventh century Latin and Greek had ceased to be mutually intelligible, the Filioque had been inserted in the West, and the papal claim had hardened into a jurisdiction the East would not concede."],christology:"One person in two natures, each complete, each retaining its properties, united without confusion in the hypostasis of the Word — with the sixth century adding that this one hypostasis is the pre-existent Word himself (the neo-Chalcedonian clarification), and the seventh adding that two natures entail two wills and two operations, humanly consenting to the divine.",today:null},{slug:"catholic-church",name:"The Catholic Church",selfName:"the Catholic Church",exonym:"Roman Catholic; Papist",era:"1054 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Chalcedonian imperial church, Latin half",origin:"The Latin West after the estrangement from Constantinople: the largest single institution in Christian history and, for most of a millennium, the only church in western Europe. Its distinguishing claim was fully formed before the schism — that the bishop of Rome, as successor of Peter, holds not merely a primacy of honour but a jurisdiction over the whole church. The Gregorian reform of the eleventh century made that claim operative against emperors and kings; the Fourth Lateran Council of 1215 gave the West transubstantiation and annual confession; Trent, meeting between 1545 and 1563, answered the Reformation by defining justification, the canon and the sacraments. The First Vatican Council defined papal infallibility in 1870, in a narrowly specified form that its opponents and its enthusiasts have both regularly overstated. The Second Vatican Council, from 1962 to 1965, permitted the vernacular liturgy and redefined the church's relation to other Christians and to Judaism.",distinctives:["The papacy: a universal jurisdiction, and an infallibility restricted by the 1870 definition to solemn definitions on faith and morals proposed for belief by the whole church, invoked explicitly perhaps once since.","Seven sacraments, transubstantiation, a celibate male priesthood in the Latin rite, and a developed canon law — the oldest continuously functioning legal system in Europe.","Twenty-four churches sui iuris, not one: the Latin Church and twenty-three Eastern Catholic churches — Maronite, Ukrainian Greek, Syro-Malabar, Melkite, Chaldean and others — with their own rites, canon law and married clergy. Many arose from reunions that left the parent church divided, which is why they remain a sore point in ecumenical dialogue.","A doctrine of development: what Newman argued and Vatican II assumed, that the deposit of faith is unfolded rather than merely repeated — the hinge on which most internal Catholic argument turns.","The clerical sexual abuse crisis, exposed from the 1980s onward across Ireland, the United States, Australia, Germany, Chile and beyond, involving both the abuse and its concealment by bishops. It is the gravest institutional failure in the church's modern history and no account of its present standing can omit it.","The 1999 Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification with the Lutheran World Federation, signed at Augsburg on the anniversary of the Ninety-Five Theses, declared a consensus on basic truths of justification such that the sixteenth-century condemnations no longer apply to the teaching set out there."],christology:"Fully Chalcedonian, with the Latin refinements of the medieval schools: one divine person subsisting in two natures, and a theology of grace, merit and satisfaction developed by Augustine, Anselm and Aquinas that Protestants would later contest at every joint.",today:"About 1.4 billion baptised members by the church's own returns — a count of baptisms recorded, not of practice, and the two diverge enormously by region. The centre of gravity has moved south: Brazil, Mexico, the Philippines, the Democratic Republic of Congo and Nigeria now weigh more than Europe, where Mass attendance in the old heartlands has collapsed. Pope Leo XIV was elected in May 2025, following the death of Pope Francis."},{slug:"eastern-orthodox",image:"73fef947d48c5a8e5ccb5932bd1a7f72.webp",imageAlt:"Orthodox clergy carrying a gold Hodegetria icon and a chalice along a wall of stripped mosaic, women following, while crusaders in red-cross tabards move among smashed furniture in the nave under the Pantocrator dome",imageCaption:"Constantinople, 1204: the Mother of God carried out past her own stripped mosaics while the Christ Pantocrator looks down on crusaders in the nave. The Fourth Crusade did more to fix the schism in place than any council, and the sack was formally apologised for only in 2004.",name:"The Eastern Orthodox Church",selfName:"the Orthodox Church",exonym:"Greek Orthodox — a Western shorthand for the whole communion, though it is the proper name only of some of its churches",era:"1054 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Chalcedonian imperial church, Greek half",origin:"The eastern half of the same imperial church, holding the seven ecumenical councils and refusing everything a council did not decide. The date 1054, when Cardinal Humbert laid a bull of excommunication on the altar of Hagia Sophia and Patriarch Michael Cerularius returned the favour, is a convenience: neither man excommunicated the other's church, and communion continued in places for a century after. Historians point instead to the accumulation of Latin novelties — the Filioque added to the creed without a council, unleavened bread, compulsory clerical celibacy, and above all the papal claim — and to the Fourth Crusade, which sacked Constantinople in 1204 and installed a Latin patriarch, as the moment the estrangement became irreparable. Reunion councils at Lyon in 1274 and Florence in 1439 were signed by emperors and repudiated by the faithful.",distinctives:["Conciliarity without a universal jurisdiction: a communion of self-governing (autocephalous) churches, with the Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople as first among equals — a primacy of honour, whose content is precisely what Orthodox and Catholics still argue about, and what Moscow and Constantinople now argue about too.","The Filioque: the West's addition of 'and the Son' to the creed's procession of the Spirit, objected to both for its content and for the manner of its insertion into a conciliar text.","Hesychasm and the theology of the divine energies, defended by Gregory Palamas and ratified at Constantinople in 1341 and 1351: God is unknowable in essence and truly participable in his uncreated energies, and deification (θέωσις) is the point of the Christian life.","Icons, defended at Nicaea II in 787 on the ground that the incarnation makes God depictable — a Christological argument, not an aesthetic one.","A married parish clergy with a monastic episcopate, and Mount Athos as a monastic republic continuously inhabited for over a thousand years.","Present divisions are jurisdictional and severe: the Council of Crete in 2016 met without four of the fourteen autocephalous churches. Moscow broke communion with Constantinople in October 2018, as Constantinople moved toward granting autocephaly to the Orthodox Church of Ukraine, and afterwards with Alexandria, Greece and Cyprus as each recognised the new church. This is the deepest intra-Orthodox rupture in centuries and it is unresolved."],christology:"Chalcedonian, expounded through the Cappadocians, Maximus the Confessor and John of Damascus, and always in service of deification: the Word became what we are so that we might become, by grace, what he is by nature. The two wills of Christ, defined in 681, matter here — a humanity without a will of its own would be a humanity that is not saved, and by extension not shared.",today:"Roughly 220 million faithful, the largest concentrations in Russia, Ukraine, Romania, Greece, Serbia, Bulgaria and Georgia, with a growing diaspora and a notable rate of convert reception in the English-speaking world. Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew I has held office since 1991. Reported adherence in traditionally Orthodox countries greatly exceeds observable practice, and the church itself says so."},{slug:"maronites",name:"The Maronite Church",selfName:"the Syriac Maronite Church of Antioch",exonym:null,era:"c. 400 – present",group:"the-council-churches",parent:"The Syriac Chalcedonian community of the Orontes valley",origin:"The Maronites take their name from Maron, a Syrian hermit who died about 410, and their institutional origin from the monastery of Bet Marun on the Orontes, whose monks were firmly Chalcedonian in a region that mostly was not. Cut off from Byzantium by the Arab conquest and harassed in the plains, the community withdrew into the mountains of Lebanon, where it elected its own patriarch — traditionally John Maron in 685 — and lived for four centuries in isolation. The Crusaders found them in 1182 and communion with Rome dates from that encounter, formalised over the following centuries, with the Maronite College founded at Rome in 1584 and the Synod of Mount Lebanon in 1736 giving the church its modern legal shape. It is the only Eastern Catholic church with no separated counterpart.",distinctives:["A West Syriac liturgy of Antiochene type, with the Anaphora of St James, retaining Syriac phrases — the words of institution among them — inside a service now largely in Arabic.","An unbroken monastic and patriarchal tradition seated at Bkerké, with the Patriarch of Antioch and All the East for the Maronites, and the Qadisha Valley's hermitages as its spiritual heart.","Historically defined by mountain refuge and by armed self-defence: the community's survival in the Levant is inseparable from Mount Lebanon's geography.","The question of a monothelite past is genuinely contested. Byzantine and crusader-era writers, including Eutychius of Alexandria and William of Tyre, report that the Maronites held one will in Christ before their union with Rome; the Maronite Church has consistently denied that it was ever formally heretical, holding the reports polemical or confused. Historians remain divided, and the church's own official position is that it has always been orthodox.","Lebanon's National Pact of 1943 reserves the presidency of the republic for a Maronite — a constitutional entrenchment that has made the church a political actor and has cost it accordingly through the civil war of 1975 to 1990 and since."],christology:"Chalcedonian and, since the union, fully aligned with Catholic dogma, including the two wills and two operations defined in 681. The historical dispute is about the seventh century, not the present.",today:"Perhaps three million Maronites worldwide, of whom well under half live in Lebanon; the diaspora in Brazil, Argentina, the United States, Australia and Canada is older and larger than the homeland community. The Lebanese economic collapse since 2019 and the Beirut port explosion of 2020 have accelerated emigration further."},{slug:"paulicians",name:"The Paulicians",selfName:"Christians",exonym:"Paulicians; Manichaeans",era:"c. 650–872, surviving in Thrace to the 12th century",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"Armenian and Anatolian Christianity",origin:"A movement of Armenian and Anatolian villagers that appears in the mid-seventh century under a leader named Constantine, who took the name Silvanus and gave his followers the names of Paul's companions — hence, probably, the label their enemies used. They rejected the visible church wholesale: its images, its cross, its sacraments, its priesthood and the veneration of Mary, keeping the gospels and Paul's letters and calling their own congregations simply Christian. Byzantine persecution alternated with toleration until the regent Theodora launched a campaign against them about 843; the chroniclers claim a hundred thousand dead, a figure that carries no evidential weight but conveys the intention. Survivors under Karbeas and his successor Chrysocheir established an independent state at Tephrike on the Arab frontier and raided Byzantine Asia Minor for a generation, until Chrysocheir was killed in 872 and Tephrike fell soon after.",distinctives:["Rejection of the material apparatus of the church: no icons, no relics, no veneration of the cross, no infant baptism, no eucharistic elements as ordinarily understood, no ordained priesthood distinct from teachers.","A canon narrowed to the gospels and Paul, with the Old Testament and Peter's letters set aside.","A military and territorial phase unusual among medieval dissenters: an armed statelet at Tephrike allied with the Abbasids against Constantinople.","The sources are the problem. Peter of Sicily's ninth-century History and related Greek treatises present them as dualists in the Manichaean line; Nina Garsoïan argued from the Armenian evidence that they were adoptionist rather than dualist, and that Greek writers filed an unfamiliar movement under a familiar heresy. The question is genuinely open.","Extinct as a movement, but consequential: successive deportations to Thrace placed a radical anti-clerical population in the Balkans a century before Bogomil appeared there."],christology:"If the Armenian sources are followed, adoptionist: Jesus an angel or a man in whom God dwelt, not the eternal Word made flesh. If the Greek sources are followed, dualist and docetic: a heavenly being who passed through Mary as through a channel and had no true human body. Both readings agree that the Paulicians denied the cross should be venerated, on the ground that the instrument of a murder is no object of worship.",today:null},{slug:"bogomils",name:"The Bogomils",selfName:"Christians; the good men",exonym:"Bogomils; Babuni; Patarenes",era:"c. 950–1463",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"Balkan Christianity, with Paulician and Messalian tributaries",origin:"About the 950s, in the newly Christianised First Bulgarian Empire, a village priest called Bogomil — 'dear to God' — began teaching that the visible world was made not by God but by his fallen elder son. Our earliest account is the Sermon Against the Heretics written by the Bulgarian presbyter Cosmas around the 970s, and it is a hostile source that is also unusually informative: he complains that the heretics are humble, pale, hard-working and refuse to eat meat, which is why people believe them. The movement spread down the trade roads to Constantinople, where Alexios I Komnenos had its leader Basil the Physician burned around 1111, and westward into Serbia, Bosnia and, by contested routes, to Lombardy and Languedoc. It was suppressed in Byzantium and Bulgaria and vanished in Bosnia after the Ottoman conquest of 1463.",distinctives:["A moderate dualism: God has two sons, and the elder, Satanael, rebelled and fashioned the material world and the human body, into which God breathed a soul. Matter is therefore the devil's work and the body a prison.","Wholesale rejection of the institutional church — buildings, crosses, icons, relics, baptism by water, the eucharist, the priesthood — and of the Old Testament apart from the Psalms and Prophets. The Lord's Prayer, said many times daily, was the whole of their liturgy.","Rigorous asceticism among the initiated: no meat, no wine, no marriage, no property, and refusal of oaths and of military service — which made them a political problem as well as a doctrinal one.","Their own literature, including the Interrogatio Iohannis (the Secret Supper), which survives because inquisitors in the West copied it as evidence.","The medieval Bosnian Church was long identified with Bogomilism in Balkan historiography. John Fine's 1975 study argued that it was instead a schismatic but doctrinally ordinary monastic church, and that reading now has wide support; the associated claim that Bosnian Bogomils converted en masse to Islam after 1463 has been largely abandoned.","Extinct, leaving the stećci — thousands of carved medieval tombstones across Bosnia and Herzegovina whose iconography is still argued over."],christology:"Docetic, so far as the sources allow a judgement: Christ is an emanation or angel of the good God who, as their opponents report, entered Mary through her ear and left a body that only appeared to be flesh, since real flesh belongs to Satanael. His work is teaching rather than atonement. Orthodox opponents replied, as against every dualism, that a Saviour who did not take a body cannot save bodies.",today:null},{slug:"cathars",name:"The Cathars",selfName:"good men and good women (bons omes, bonas femnas); good Christians",exonym:"Cathars; Albigensians",era:"c. 1140–1321",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"Contested: possibly Bogomil transmission, possibly indigenous Occitan dissent",origin:"From the middle of the twelfth century, church authorities in the Rhineland, Lombardy and above all Languedoc reported communities that rejected the sacraments, the cross and the flesh, honoured ascetic teachers called good men and good women, and were protected by local nobles. The word Cathar appears first in Eckbert of Schönau in 1163, who derived it from the Greek καθαροί, the pure; the people so labelled did not use it. Papal preaching missions failed; the murder of a papal legate in 1208 triggered the Albigensian Crusade, twenty years of war that destroyed the independence of the county of Toulouse and killed on a scale that shocked contemporaries. The Inquisition ground on for a century; Montségur fell in 1244 and two hundred of the consoled were burned below it; the last known good man, Guillaume Bélibaste, was burned in 1321.",distinctives:["A dualist account of the world, absolute in some communities (two eternal principles) and mitigated in others (a fallen creator), with the material world as the work of the evil power and the human soul as an angel trapped in flesh.","One sacrament, the consolamentum, a laying-on of hands binding the recipient thereafter to celibacy, poverty, truth-telling and a diet excluding all products of coition. Ordinary believers received it on their deathbeds. 'Perfectus' and 'perfecta' are the inquisitors' terms, not theirs.","Women could receive the consolamentum and preach, and houses of good women were centres of the movement — a standing the Latin church did not offer.","Nearly everything known comes from the records of those who destroyed them: inquisitors' registers, above all Jacques Fournier's, from which Emmanuel Le Roy Ladurie reconstructed the village of Montaillou in 1975.","The deconstruction debate is real and unresolved, and this portal will not pretend otherwise. Mark Pegg, R. I. Moore, Uwe Brunn and Julien Théry argue there was no organised dualist counter-church in the West at all — that 'Catharism' was assembled by clerics out of patristic heresiology and imposed on local dissent by the questions inquisitors asked. Against them, Peter Biller, Bernard Hamilton, Claire Taylor and Lucy Sackville point to dualist texts written by the accused themselves, including the Book of Two Principles, to Rainerius Sacconi's account by a former insider, and to surviving rituals, and hold that a real dualist church existed even if less organised and less Bogomil-derived than older scholarship supposed.","'Caedite eos. Novit enim Dominus qui sunt eius' — kill them all, God will know his own — is attributed to the legate Arnaud Amaury at Béziers in 1209 by Caesarius of Heisterbach writing a decade or more later, and is very probably legendary. The massacre it describes was not."],christology:"Docetic where the sources permit a judgement: Christ is an angelic being sent by the good God, who took only the appearance of a body, since incarnation in the devil's matter is what a good God would not do. He saves by teaching the way of return, not by dying. The Catholic objection reversed the point exactly: what is not assumed is not healed.",today:null},{slug:"waldensians",name:"The Waldensians",selfName:"the Poor of Lyon; Chiesa Evangelica Valdese",exonym:"Vaudois; Waldenses",era:"c. 1173 – present",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"The twelfth-century apostolic poverty movement",origin:"About 1173 a wealthy merchant of Lyon named Valdes gave away his property, commissioned a vernacular translation of the gospels, and began to preach. He was not a heretic and did not want to be one: he took his case to the Third Lateran Council in 1179 and was permitted to keep his poverty but forbidden to preach without episcopal licence. He preached anyway, on the ground that one must obey God rather than men, and in 1184 the Poor of Lyon were excommunicated at Verona. The offence was not doctrine but unlicensed lay preaching — including by women — and the possession of scripture in a language people understood. Driven into the Alpine valleys of Piedmont and Dauphiné, they survived three centuries of persecution as an underground church served by itinerant preachers called barbes, and in 1532, at the Synod of Chanforan, they joined the Reformation and adopted Reformed theology, funding Olivétan's French Bible out of their own collections.",distinctives:["Vernacular scripture and lay preaching as the founding claim, three and a half centuries before Luther.","Refusal of oaths, of the death penalty and of purgatory, prayers for the dead and indulgences — positions reached independently and long before the Reformation articulated them.","The Piedmontese Easter of 1655, when the Duke of Savoy's troops massacred the valley communities, provoking Cromwell's intervention and Milton's sonnet with its opening plea, 'Avenge, O Lord, thy slaughter'd saints'.","The Glorious Return of 1689, when about a thousand exiles fought their way back from Geneva into the valleys under Henri Arnaud, and the Letters Patent of Charles Albert of 17 February 1848, which granted them civil rights and which the church still celebrates with bonfires.","Union with the Italian Methodists in 1979 to form the Unione delle chiese metodiste e valdesi, and a theological faculty in Rome.","In June 2015 Pope Francis visited the Waldensian temple in Turin and asked forgiveness for the church's historic persecution of them — the first pope ever to do so."],christology:"Reformed since 1532 and Chalcedonian throughout: their medieval quarrel with Rome was about preaching, scripture and the moral authority of a wealthy clergy, never about the person of Christ.",today:"Small and disproportionately audible. Around twenty to thirty thousand members in Italy, with the Iglesia Evangélica Valdense del Río de la Plata in Uruguay and Argentina adding perhaps thirteen thousand more. The Italian church funds an extensive social and refugee programme from its share of the state's otto per mille tax allocation, most of which is given by people who are not Waldensians. Of the dissenting movements condemned in the medieval West, it is the one that survived intact into the Reformation and remains an organised church today."},{slug:"lollards",name:"The Lollards",selfName:"true men; known men",exonym:"Lollards (from Middle Dutch lollaerd, 'mumbler')",era:"c. 1382 – 16th century",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"The Oxford theology of John Wyclif",origin:"John Wyclif, an Oxford philosopher of great subtlety and considerable political usefulness to the English crown, argued in the 1370s and 1380s that dominion is founded on grace, that a church in mortal sin has no title to its property, that scripture is the sole authority, and finally that the substance of bread remains after consecration. That last position lost him his protectors. His followers, clerical at first and then increasingly artisan and lay, produced the first complete English Bibles in the 1380s and 1390s and the Twelve Conclusions posted at Westminster in 1395. Repression was rapid and, by English standards, novel: the statute De heretico comburendo of 1401 made burning the penalty, and Sir John Oldcastle's rising of 1414 turned Lollardy into treason. The Council of Constance condemned Wyclif in 1415, and in 1428 his bones were dug up and burned and the ashes thrown into the Swift.",distinctives:["Scripture in English, copied and circulated illegally; possession of an English gospel was itself evidence of heresy under Arundel's Constitutions, drawn up in 1407 and published in 1409.","Rejection of transubstantiation in favour of 'remanence' — the bread remains bread while being sacramentally Christ's body — along with rejection of pilgrimages, images, clerical celibacy and the church's temporal wealth.","Lay preaching, including by women, and a conviction that a priest in mortal sin cannot validly minister — the Donatist question again.","Survival underground in the Chilterns, Essex, Kent and the Midlands through the fifteenth century, sustained by book-lending networks among weavers and tradesmen rather than by clergy.","Whether Lollardy prepared England for the Reformation is a long-running historiographical fight. A. G. Dickens argued that it provided a receptive substratum; revisionists including Eamon Duffy and Christopher Haigh replied that late medieval English Catholicism was vigorous and popular, and that the Reformation was imposed from above. The current position is a qualified middle.","Wyclif's writings, carried to Bohemia through the Anglo-Bohemian court connections of Richard II's marriage, shaped Jan Hus directly — the movement's most consequential legacy."],christology:"Orthodox and Chalcedonian. The Lollard quarrel was with the eucharist, the priesthood, the church's wealth and the language of the Bible, not with the creed.",today:null},{slug:"hussites-moravians",name:"The Hussites and the Moravians",selfName:"Unitas Fratrum (Jednota bratrská); the Unity of the Brethren",exonym:"Hussites; Utraquists; Picards",era:"1415 – present",group:"medieval-dissent",parent:"Bohemian reform, and John Wyclif's Oxford theology",origin:"Jan Hus, preaching in Czech at the Bethlehem Chapel in Prague, took up Wyclif's criticisms of clerical wealth and authority and was burned at the Council of Constance on 6 July 1415 in defiance of an imperial safe-conduct. Bohemia rose. The Four Articles of Prague in 1420 demanded free preaching, communion in both kinds for the laity, the poverty of the clergy and the punishment of public sin, and under the general Jan Žižka, blind from 1421, the Hussites defeated five crusades. Moderates and radicals then destroyed each other: the Utraquists defeated the Taborites at Lipany in 1434 and secured the Compacta of Basel in 1436, permitting Bohemia its lay chalice — a concession revoked by Pius II in 1462. Out of the disillusioned radical remnant, and the pacifist writings of Petr Chelčický, came the Unity of the Brethren, founded at Kunvald in 1457.",distinctives:["Communion in both kinds for the laity — sub utraque specie, the chalice that gives the Utraquists their name — as the movement's emblem and battle standard, a century before Luther.","Preaching and scripture in Czech, and the Kralice Bible of 1579 to 1593, which did for the Czech language what Luther's Bible did for German.","The Taborite wing: chiliastic, iconoclastic, communal in its early phase, and militarily innovative — Žižka's war-wagons defeated armoured cavalry repeatedly and were studied across Europe.","The Unity of the Brethren, from 1457, with its own bishops, the first printed vernacular hymnal in Christendom (1501, sixteen years before Luther's theses), and Comenius, its last bishop in exile and one of the founders of modern pedagogy, before the Habsburg victory at the White Mountain in 1620 destroyed Protestant Bohemia.","Renewal at Herrnhut on Count Zinzendorf's Saxon estate from 1722, and the spiritual breakthrough of 13 August 1727 that Moravians call their Pentecost; a round-the-clock prayer watch was begun that the tradition holds ran for a hundred years.","Missions from 1732 — the West Indies, Greenland, Suriname, South Africa, Labrador — the first sustained Protestant missionary enterprise, undertaken by a body of a few hundred people. It was at a Moravian society meeting in Aldersgate Street on 24 May 1738 that John Wesley's heart was, in his words, strangely warmed."],christology:"Orthodox and Chalcedonian throughout, with a Moravian devotional concentration on the wounds and blood of Christ that reached extravagant expression in the 1740s 'sifting time' and was afterwards moderated. Hussite theology's distinctive weight fell on the eucharist and on the church, not on the person of Christ.",today:"The Moravian Church numbers roughly a million worldwide, and its largest provinces are in Tanzania rather than Europe or America. In the Czech lands the Utraquist church was extinguished after 1620; the modern Czechoslovak Hussite Church, founded in 1920, and the Evangelical Church of Czech Brethren, from 1918, are twentieth-century bodies claiming the inheritance rather than continuations of it."},{slug:"lutherans",name:"The Lutheran Churches",selfName:"Evangelical; Evangelical-Lutheran",exonym:"Lutherans (Luther's own objection: he wanted no church named after him)",era:"1517 – present",group:"the-reformations",parent:"The Latin Catholic Church",origin:"Martin Luther, an Augustinian friar and professor at Wittenberg, published ninety-five theses against indulgences on 31 October 1517 — whether he also nailed them to the castle church door is disputed, since the story rests on Melanchthon's account written after Luther's death, and Erwin Iserloh's challenge to it in 1961 has never been fully answered. The theses were a scholastic protest against a financial abuse; within three years Luther was arguing that the church is constituted by the gospel rather than the papacy, that there are two sacraments rather than seven, and that a Christian is justified by faith alone. Excommunicated in 1521 and outlawed at the Diet of Worms in the same year, he was sheltered by his prince, translated the New Testament into German in eleven weeks, and spent the rest of his life building a church he had not intended to found.",distinctives:["Justification by grace through faith alone, on account of Christ alone, as the article by which the church stands or falls — and the correlative distinction between law and gospel as the key to reading scripture. The Augsburg Confession of 1530 remains the charter; the Book of Concord of 1580 collected the confessional documents.","The real presence understood as sacramental union: the true body and blood are given in, with and under the bread and wine. Luther broke with Zwingli over this at Marburg in 1529, and the breach split Protestantism permanently in its first decade.","A conservative liturgical and musical reformation: vestments, the church year and the chorale retained, and a musical tradition running to Bach.","Territorial establishment under the Peace of Augsburg of 1555, later summarised in the formula cuius regio, eius religio — a phrase coined only in the following century — which made confession a function of princely choice.","Luther's late writings against the Jews, above all On the Jews and Their Lies of 1543, are vicious, were used by the Nazis, and are repudiated: the Lutheran World Federation formally rejected them in 1984, and member churches have issued their own repudiations. No honest Lutheran self-description omits this.","The Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification, signed with the Catholic Church at Augsburg on 31 October 1999 and subsequently affirmed by the World Methodist Council, the Anglican Consultative Council and the World Communion of Reformed Churches."],christology:"Chalcedonian, with a distinctive emphasis on the communication of attributes: because the two natures are united in one person, what belongs to the divinity may be predicated of the humanity, which is how Luther defends the bodily presence of Christ in the supper wherever it is celebrated. Reformed theologians objected that this compromises the integrity of the human nature; the argument over the so-called extra Calvinisticum has never been settled between them.",today:"Somewhere between seventy and ninety million Lutherans worldwide depending on what is counted; the Lutheran World Federation reports about seventy-seven million in its member churches. Its two largest member churches are now in Ethiopia and Tanzania, ahead of the Church of Sweden, and nominal membership in Germany and Scandinavia remains high while practice is very low. Confessional bodies outside the LWF, notably the Lutheran Church–Missouri Synod and the International Lutheran Council, differ sharply from it on scripture, ordination and ecumenism."},{slug:"reformed-presbyterian",name:"The Reformed and Presbyterian Churches",selfName:"Reformed; the Kirk; Presbyterian",exonym:"Calvinist; Huguenot",era:"1519 – present",group:"the-reformations",parent:"The Latin Catholic Church, through Zurich and Geneva",origin:"Huldrych Zwingli began reforming Zurich in 1519 on humanist and biblical grounds, independently of Luther and to more radical effect: images removed, the Mass abolished, the eucharist read as memorial. He was killed in battle at Kappel in 1531. John Calvin, a French lawyer exiled for his faith, published the Institutes of the Christian Religion in 1536 at the age of twenty-six and spent most of the rest of his life organising Geneva, whose consistory, academy and printing presses made it the training ground and supply depot of an international movement. From Geneva the Reformed faith went to France as the Huguenot churches, to the Netherlands, to Scotland with John Knox, to the Palatinate, to Hungary and Poland, and to England as Puritanism. Its instrument was not a prince but a confession and a polity: elders and synods, exportable anywhere.",distinctives:["The sovereignty of God and the doctrine of election, answered against the Dutch Remonstrants at the Synod of Dort in 1618 and 1619 in canons remembered since as the five points.","Covenant theology, binding the Testaments into one story of promise, and the regulative principle of worship: what scripture does not command in worship is not to be done, which is why Reformed churches stripped their walls.","A spiritual real presence in the supper — Christ is truly received by faith through the Spirit, not locally enclosed in the elements — Calvin's mediating position between Luther and Zwingli, which satisfied neither.","Government by elders in graded courts: session, presbytery, synod, general assembly. It proved a template for representative institutions well beyond the church, and it is the reason Presbyterians have divided so precisely and so often.","A dense confessional literature: the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563, the Belgic Confession, the Second Helvetic Confession, the Scots Confession of 1560, and the Westminster Confession and Catechisms of the 1640s.","Two things Reformed churches now name against themselves: Geneva's burning of Michael Servetus for antitrinitarianism in 1553, which Calvin had pressed for and approved, and the theological defence of apartheid by the Dutch Reformed Church of South Africa, answered from within the tradition by the Belhar Confession of 1986."],christology:"Chalcedonian, with the insistence — the extra Calvinisticum — that the eternal Word, while wholly united to the assumed humanity, is not confined within it. Reformed Christology carries a strong doctrine of Christ's threefold office as prophet, priest and king, and of his continuing human mediation at the Father's right hand.",today:"The World Communion of Reformed Churches claims about one hundred million people in over a hundred countries, and other counts of Reformed and Presbyterian bodies run lower, around sixty to eighty million; the difference is largely a matter of which united churches are included. The largest single Presbyterian communities are now in South Korea, and the tradition is strong in Indonesia, Nigeria, Ghana and Brazil. In its European heartlands it has contracted severely."},{slug:"old-believers",name:"The Old Believers",selfName:"старообрядцы (staroobryadtsy) — Old Ritualists",exonym:"раскольники (raskolniki) — schismatics",era:"1666 – present",group:"the-reformations",parent:"The Russian Orthodox Church",origin:"This is not a Reformation and should not be filed as one; it belongs here by date and by the shape of the question, which is who has authority to change what the faithful have received. Patriarch Nikon, from 1653, revised the Russian service books and usages to conform to contemporary Greek practice: the sign of the cross made with three fingers instead of two, the name of Jesus spelled Иисус instead of Исус, three alleluias instead of two, processions counter-sunwise. To Russians who believed Moscow the Third Rome and their own usages the pure inheritance, this was not tidying but apostasy. The Great Moscow Council of 1666 and 1667 anathematised the old rites and those who kept them — and deposed Nikon too, having accepted his reforms. The Archpriest Avvakum, whose autobiography is the first masterpiece of Russian prose, was burned at Pustozersk in 1682. Thousands burned themselves alive rather than accept the new books.",distinctives:["The two-fingered sign of the cross, the old spelling of the Saviour's name, the eight-pointed cross, the pre-Nikonian books, znamenny chant sung from neumes, and the old icon canon — all kept unaltered for three and a half centuries.","A division forced by having no bishops: the popovtsy, who sought priests and eventually obtained a hierarchy at Belaya Krinitsa in 1846, and the bespopovtsy, who concluded that the age of grace-bearing priesthood had ended and live without eucharist or ordained clergy.","Two and a half centuries of legal disability, double taxation and periodic persecution, ended only by Nicholas II's edict of toleration in April 1905 — a brief golden age closed by the Bolsheviks.","Emigration to the far edges: Romania's Lipovans, the Baltic communities, and settlements in Alaska, Oregon, Alberta, Brazil and Bolivia, several of which still speak an archaic Russian.","The Russian Orthodox Church's Local Council of 1971 formally lifted the seventeenth-century anathemas and declared the old rites equally salvific — an admission, three hundred years late, that the schism had been made over nothing that needed to divide.","Old Believer communities preserved manuscripts, icons and choral traditions the state church had discarded; much of what is known of medieval Russian chant and iconography survives because they refused to modernise."],christology:"Entirely Orthodox and Chalcedonian, identical to that of the church they left. The Old Believer schism is the clearest case in the family of a rupture over rite and authority in which no article of the creed was ever in dispute — which is precisely what made it so bitter, and what made the 1971 lifting of the anathemas so difficult to explain.",today:"Perhaps one to two million worldwide, most in Russia, with communities in Romania, Latvia, Lithuania, Moldova, the United States and South America. The Russian Orthodox Old-Rite Church (the Belokrinitsa hierarchy) is the largest priested body; the Pomortsy the largest priestless one. Some Old Rite parishes now exist within the Moscow Patriarchate itself under the edinoverie arrangement."},{slug:"old-catholics",name:"The Old Catholic Churches",selfName:"the Union of Utrecht of the Old Catholic Churches",exonym:null,era:"1724 / 1870 – present",group:"the-reformations",parent:"The Catholic Church",origin:"Two roots. The first is Dutch: the Chapter of Utrecht, having quarrelled with Rome over Jansenism and over its right to elect its own archbishop, elected Cornelius Steenoven in 1723 and had him consecrated on 15 October 1724 by the missionary bishop Dominique Marie Varlet, without papal mandate, maintaining thereafter a small canonically irregular but sacramentally valid church for a century and a half. The second is German and Swiss: when the First Vatican Council defined papal infallibility on 18 July 1870, a body of Catholic academics and laity led by the church historian Ignaz von Döllinger refused to accept it as an authentic development of the apostolic faith. Döllinger was excommunicated in 1871, a congress at Munich organised the dissenters, and in 1873 the Deventer bishop of the Utrecht line consecrated Joseph Hubert Reinkens for them — which is how the two roots became one tree. The Declaration of Utrecht of 1889 constituted the Union.",distinctives:["Catholic in sacraments, orders, liturgy and episcopal succession; the objection is precisely and narrowly to papal universal jurisdiction and infallibility as defined in 1870.","The appeal to the undivided church of the first millennium and to the canon of Vincent of Lérins — what has been believed everywhere, always and by all — as the test of doctrine.","Synodal government with lay and clerical participation in electing bishops, a vernacular liturgy from the 1870s and 1880s, and no obligatory clerical celibacy.","The Bonn Agreement of 1931 established full communion with the Anglican Communion, the first such agreement between Anglicans and a church of the Western Catholic tradition.","The ordination of women to the priesthood from the 1990s in the German, Swiss, Austrian and Dutch churches led the Polish National Catholic Church to withdraw from the Union of Utrecht in 2003.","A caution about the name: a large number of unrelated independent bodies and episcopi vagantes trade as 'Old Catholic' without any connection to the Union of Utrecht, which recognises only its own member churches."],christology:"Wholly Catholic and Chalcedonian; nothing in the Old Catholic separation touches the creed. It is a dispute about the constitution of the church and about what may be defined as revealed, conducted by people who continued to say the same Mass.",today:"Very small — the Union of Utrecht's churches together number in the tens of thousands, with the Netherlands, Germany, Switzerland and Austria as the historic provinces, and the Polish National Catholic Church of North America, now outside the Union, adding perhaps twenty-five thousand. Their ecumenical significance has always exceeded their size."},{slug:"anabaptists",name:"The Anabaptists",selfName:"Brethren; Taufgesinnte (the baptism-minded)",exonym:"Anabaptists — 'rebaptisers', a capital charge under Roman law revived in 1529",era:"1525 – present",group:"the-radical-reformation",parent:"The Zurich reformation of Zwingli",origin:"On 21 January 1525, in a house in Zurich, Conrad Grebel baptised George Blaurock on his confession of faith, and Blaurock baptised the others present. They were Zwingli's own students, and they had concluded that his reformation stopped short: if scripture alone governs the church, then infant baptism has no warrant, and a church made of everyone born in a canton is not a church at all. Zurich responded by drowning Felix Manz in the Limmat in January 1527, a punishment chosen for its symbolism. The Schleitheim Articles of the same year, drafted by Michael Sattler shortly before he was executed, set out what the movement would become. Then came Münster, where in 1534 and 1535 a millenarian faction seized a city, installed a king, introduced polygamy and was destroyed — an episode wholly unlike the rest of the movement that nonetheless defined its reputation for four centuries.",distinctives:["Baptism on confession of faith, and with it a church of voluntary members rather than of everyone born within a territory — the single idea from which most modern free-church Christianity descends.","Separation of church and state, held not as a civic principle but as a spiritual one: a church that borrows the magistrate's sword has lost the cross.","Nonresistance, the refusal of oaths, and discipline by the ban rather than by the courts.","Persecution by Catholics and Protestants alike on a scale that makes them the most-executed Christian movement of the sixteenth century; the Martyrs Mirror of 1660 records more than 4,000 names and remains a household book among the descendants.","Münster is the movement's permanent embarrassment and its permanent lesson. The mainstream Anabaptist tradition after 1535 was defined by an explicit repudiation of it, and the pacifism of Menno Simons was formed in its shadow.","'The Radical Reformation' as a category is George Huntston Williams's, from 1962; it gathers Anabaptists with spiritualists and antitrinitarians who had rather less in common with them than the label suggests."],christology:"Orthodox and Chalcedonian in effect, though several early leaders — Menno Simons among them — held a 'celestial flesh' Christology in which Christ's body came through Mary rather than from her, a view later abandoned by their descendants. The tradition's real theological centre is not the person of Christ but discipleship: Nachfolge, the claim that following Jesus in life is inseparable from believing in him.",today:"About 2.1 million baptised members are counted by Mennonite World Conference across the Anabaptist family, in 86 countries, together with the Amish, Hutterites, Brethren in Christ and Church of the Brethren traditions. The majority of Anabaptists now live in Africa, Asia and Latin America; Ethiopia's Meserete Kristos Church is the largest single national body."},{slug:"mennonites-amish",name:"The Mennonites and the Amish",selfName:"Mennonites; Amish (Amische); the plain people",exonym:null,era:"1536 / 1693 – present",group:"the-radical-reformation",parent:"The Anabaptists",origin:"Menno Simons was a Frisian parish priest who left the Roman church in 1536, a year after Münster, and spent twenty-five years as a hunted itinerant gathering the scattered and terrified Dutch Anabaptists into peaceable congregations. His name attached to them. Their history is one of migration: from the Netherlands and Switzerland to Prussia, from Prussia to the Russian steppe on Catherine the Great's invitation of 1789, from Russia to Kansas, Manitoba and Paraguay when military exemption was withdrawn, and from all of these to Latin America. The Amish are a division within this story rather than outside it: in 1693 the Alsatian elder Jakob Ammann broke with the Swiss Brethren over the strictness of shunning and the practice of footwashing, and his followers, migrating to Pennsylvania from the 1730s, became the most visible plain community in North America.",distinctives:["Believers' baptism, nonresistance and refusal of oaths, with a congregational discipline enforced by the ban and, among the Amish, by Meidung — social avoidance of the excommunicated, the very issue on which the 1693 division turned.","Amish Ordnung: an unwritten community rule governing dress, technology and conduct, decided by each church district, which is why the horse and buggy in one settlement coexists with a diesel generator in the next.","Pennsylvania Dutch, a Palatine German dialect, as the everyday language of most Old Order Amish, with High German for scripture and English for the outside world.","Wisconsin v. Yoder (1972), in which the United States Supreme Court held that compulsory schooling beyond the eighth grade violated Amish free exercise — the leading American case on religious exemption.","The forgiveness extended by the Nickel Mines community to the family of the man who murdered five of their schoolgirls in 2006 drew international attention to a practice the tradition regards as unremarkable.","The Amish population of North America has grown from a few thousand a century ago to roughly 400,000, doubling about every twenty years — one of the fastest-growing religious populations in the West, entirely by retention rather than conversion."],christology:"Chalcedonian and unelaborated. These are traditions of practice rather than of dogmatic construction, and they have generally resisted confessional systematising; the Dordrecht Confession of 1632 remains the Amish standard and is short.",today:"Mennonite World Conference counts about 2.1 million baptised members worldwide, with the largest bodies in Ethiopia, the Democratic Republic of Congo, India, the United States and Indonesia — most Mennonites are neither white nor North American, which surprises most North Americans. Old Order Amish number around 400,000 across some 32 states and Ontario, with large settlements in Pennsylvania, Ohio and Indiana."},{slug:"hutterites",name:"The Hutterites",selfName:"Hutterischen Brüder; the Brethren",exonym:null,era:"1528 – present",group:"the-radical-reformation",parent:"The Anabaptists",origin:"Among Anabaptist refugees gathered in Moravia in 1528, a group spread a cloak on the ground and laid their possessions on it, and community of goods has been the defining mark of their descendants ever since. Jakob Hutter, a Tyrolean hatmaker, organised the communities between 1533 and his execution at Innsbruck in 1536, and gave them his name. Moravia's tolerant nobility allowed a golden period from the 1550s to the 1590s in which perhaps twenty to thirty thousand Hutterites lived in Bruderhöfe renowned for their craftsmanship, medicine and schools. The Thirty Years' War ended it. Thereafter: Transylvania, Wallachia, Russia in 1770, and in 1874 emigration to Dakota Territory, from which every Hutterite alive today descends.",distinctives:["Community of goods (Gütergemeinschaft) held as a command rather than an ideal, on the model of Acts 2 and 4 — no private property, all income to the colony, all needs met from it. It is the longest continuously practised communal economy in the Western world.","Colony life: a communal kitchen, a German school and an English school, work assigned by the colony, and division of a colony into two when it exceeds roughly 130 to 150 people — a deliberate ceiling on scale.","Three Leut — Schmiedeleut, Dariusleut and Lehrerleut — descending from the three shiploads that reached Dakota in the 1870s, with a fourth group, the Prairieleut, who took up private farms and were largely absorbed into other churches.","The Chronicle, a communal history kept from the sixteenth century onward, and a hymn tradition of martyr ballads sung in a sixteenth-century German.","During the First World War four Hutterite conscientious objectors were imprisoned in the United States; two, Joseph and Michael Hofer, died at Fort Leavenworth in 1918, after which almost the entire population moved to Canada.","The Bruderhof, founded in Germany in 1920 by Eberhard Arnold, is a separate communal movement that has associated with and separated from the Hutterites more than once and should not be confused with them."],christology:"Chalcedonian and confessionally Anabaptist, expressed in Peter Riedemann's Account of Our Religion, written in prison about 1540 and printed in 1565, and largely unrevised since. As with the Amish, the theological weight falls on the shape of the common life rather than on dogmatic elaboration.",today:"Roughly 50,000 people in about 500 colonies, almost all in Manitoba, Alberta and Saskatchewan and in the northern plains states of the United States. The population grows steadily by birth; retention is high but no longer universal, and colonies now negotiate the internet as earlier generations negotiated the telephone."},{slug:"unitarians",name:"The Unitarians",selfName:"Unitarian; Socinian (historically); Unitarian Universalist",exonym:"Antitrinitarians; Arians — the second is a misnomer, since Socinians denied the pre-existence that Arians affirmed",era:"1531 – present",group:"the-radical-reformation",parent:"The Radical Reformation, by way of Poland and Transylvania",origin:"If scripture alone is the authority, some sixteenth-century readers concluded, then the word Trinity is not in it. Michael Servetus published On the Errors of the Trinity in 1531 and was burned at Geneva in 1553, an execution that shocked even contemporaries and made toleration an argument. In Poland the Minor Reformed Church separated in 1565 and, under the Italian exile Fausto Sozzini, developed Socinianism and produced the Racovian Catechism of 1605 before being expelled from the country in 1658. In Transylvania the preacher Ferenc Dávid persuaded King John Sigismund, and the Diet of Torda in 1568 issued one of Europe's earliest legal guarantees of religious toleration; the Hungarian Unitarian Church founded there still exists. In England antitrinitarianism remained a felony until the Doctrine of the Trinity Act of 1813; in New England it captured much of Congregationalism, and William Ellery Channing's Baltimore sermon of 1819 gave it a name and a manifesto.",distinctives:["The unipersonality of God, and Jesus as the man in whom God acted — argued, in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, entirely from the biblical text against the creeds.","Socinianism went further than later liberal Unitarianism in some respects and less far in others: it denied Christ's pre-existence, original sin and substitutionary atonement while retaining the virgin birth, miracles and the resurrection, and it held that the risen Christ is now to be worshipped.","The Edict of Torda of 1568 and the Racovian Academy stand at the head of the European argument for freedom of conscience, which antitrinitarians made because they needed it.","In nineteenth-century America the tradition moved from biblical unitarianism to a broad religious liberalism, through Emerson and the Transcendentalists, and merged with the Universalists in 1961 to form the Unitarian Universalist Association.","This produced two quite different bodies that share a name, and honesty requires distinguishing them. The Hungarian and Transylvanian Unitarian churches, and the British General Assembly in part, remain explicitly Christian, keeping the Lord's Supper and preaching from the gospels. The Unitarian Universalist Association in North America is deliberately post-creedal and pluralist; many of its congregations no longer describe themselves as Christian, and it does not ask them to.","The tradition's social record is disproportionate to its size: Priestley in science and dissent, Martineau, and in America the antislavery and women's rights movements."],christology:"Explicitly non-Nicene. Christ is understood as a man uniquely commissioned and empowered by God — in classical Socinianism, a man exalted to divine authority after the resurrection; in modern liberal Unitarianism, more often a teacher and exemplar. Trinitarian churches judge this to place the tradition outside the confession that defines them, and Unitarians accept the description without accepting the judgement: their point has always been that the confession itself was the innovation.",today:"Small everywhere. The Unitarian Universalist Association reports around 150,000 members in North America and has been declining; the Hungarian Unitarian Church in Transylvania and Hungary numbers perhaps 60,000 to 75,000; the British General Assembly is a few thousand. There are also Unitarian congregations in the Khasi Hills of north-east India, founded in 1887 and unconnected to any Western mission."},{slug:"anglicans",name:"The Anglican Communion",selfName:"the Church of England; Anglican; Episcopal",exonym:null,era:"1534 – present",group:"the-english-speaking-explosion",parent:"The Catholic Church in England",origin:"The English Reformation began as a jurisdictional act — Henry VIII's Act of Supremacy of 1534, occasioned by a marriage — and became a theological one under his son, when Thomas Cranmer produced the Books of Common Prayer of 1549 and 1552 and gave English Protestantism a liturgy of extraordinary durability and prose. Mary I restored Rome and burned Cranmer; Elizabeth I settled the matter in 1559 with an establishment broad enough to hold most of the country and precise enough to exclude Rome, defined by the Prayer Book, the Thirty-Nine Articles and the queen's supremacy. Richard Hooker gave it a theology of scripture, tradition and reason. The Civil War abolished it and the Restoration brought it back with the Prayer Book of 1662, whose Act of Uniformity ejected some two thousand Puritan ministers and created English Nonconformity at a stroke. The Oxford Movement from 1833 recovered its Catholic self-understanding; empire and the American Revolution made it a communion of independent provinces rather than a national church.",distinctives:["Common prayer rather than a confession as the instrument of unity: Anglicans have been held together by praying the same words far more than by agreeing on what the words mean.","A deliberately comprehensive settlement containing Catholic, Reformed and liberal parties — though the description of it as a via media between Rome and Geneva is largely a nineteenth-century Tractarian reading projected backwards, and most historians now read the Elizabethan church as understanding itself straightforwardly as a Reformed church.","Episcopal order retained without a papacy, and the Chicago-Lambeth Quadrilateral of 1886 and 1888 offering scripture, the two creeds, the two dominical sacraments and the historic episcopate as the basis of reunion.","A communion of forty-two autonomous provinces bound by four instruments — the Archbishop of Canterbury, the Lambeth Conference from 1867, the Primates' Meeting and the Anglican Consultative Council — none of which can legislate for another province. This is its constitution and its present difficulty.","Women were ordained priest in Hong Kong in 1944 out of wartime necessity, regularised from 1971, admitted to the English priesthood in 1994 and to the English episcopate by legislation in 2014, the first consecrated in 2015; provinces differ, and some still do not ordain women.","The Communion is in a state of impaired communion over sexuality. The consecration of Gene Robinson in 2003, the formation of GAFCON in 2008 and of the Anglican Church in North America in 2009, the boycott of Lambeth 2022, and the Church of England's authorisation of Prayers of Love and Faith in 2023 have led a majority of the world's practising Anglicans — chiefly in Nigeria, Uganda, Rwanda and Kenya — to declare that they no longer recognise Canterbury's leadership. Any account that describes this as settled is describing something else."],christology:"Chalcedonian, held through the creeds and the Articles rather than through a distinctive Anglican formulation; the tradition's theological energy has gone into ecclesiology, sacraments and the relation of church to nation.",today:"About 85 million baptised members claimed across the Communion, with by far the largest provinces in Nigeria and Uganda; the Church of England, which reports usual Sunday attendance well under 700,000, is now a small body inside a large communion, and the disparity is the engine of most current conflict."},{slug:"baptists",name:"The Baptists",selfName:"Baptists",exonym:"Anabaptists (a slur their opponents used and they rejected)",era:"1609 – present",group:"the-english-speaking-explosion",parent:"English Separatism, with Dutch Mennonite contact",origin:"A congregation of English Separatists exiled in Amsterdam concluded in 1609 that a church of confessing believers required believers' baptism; their pastor John Smyth baptised himself and then the rest. Thomas Helwys led part of the group back to England and founded the first Baptist church on English soil at Spitalfields in 1612, publishing the same year a plea for liberty of conscience that extended it to heretics, Jews and Muslims and to the king's own subjects — an argument for which he died in Newgate. A second stream, the Particular Baptists, emerged from Calvinist Separatism in the late 1630s and adopted immersion; the First London Confession of 1644 is theirs. Roger Williams, banished from Massachusetts, founded Providence and the first Baptist church in America around 1638. Through the eighteenth- and nineteenth-century revivals, Baptists became the largest Protestant family in the United States.",distinctives:["Baptism of believers by immersion, and a regenerate church membership; baptism follows profession rather than birth.","The autonomy of the local congregation, associating voluntarily rather than being governed from above — which is why Baptists have conventions and unions but no bishops and no binding synods.","Liberty of conscience and the separation of church and state as a founding Baptist commitment, not a later American borrowing: Helwys in 1612 and Williams in the 1640s made the case before any state adopted it.","No creed but the Bible, in principle — although Baptists have produced a great many confessions, and the tension between confessional identity and congregational freedom has caused most of their divisions.","Two American histories in one family: the Southern Baptist Convention was founded in 1845 in defence of slaveholding missionaries and apologised for it in 1995, while the Black Baptist churches and the National Baptist Convention became the institutional base of the civil rights movement and of Martin Luther King Jr, himself a Baptist pastor.","Baptists carried the modern Protestant missionary movement's opening act — William Carey to Bengal in 1793 — and the tradition is now strongest outside the West, in Nigeria, India, Brazil, Myanmar and the Democratic Republic of Congo."],christology:"Chalcedonian and, in most Baptist confessions, explicitly so. The family divides internally between Calvinist and Arminian soteriologies — the historic Particular and General Baptist streams — a difference that runs through Baptist life to the present day.",today:"The Baptist World Alliance counts around 51 million members in 128 countries; adding the Southern Baptist Convention, which left the Alliance in 2004 and reports about 13 million, and the many unaffiliated Baptist bodies, total Baptist membership is usually placed somewhere between 75 and 100 million, with adherent counts higher still. American Baptist membership has been declining for two decades while African and Asian Baptist churches grow."},{slug:"quakers",name:"The Religious Society of Friends",selfName:"Friends; Friends in the Truth",exonym:"Quakers — originally a magistrate's taunt, later adopted",era:"1652 – present",group:"the-english-speaking-explosion",parent:"The English radical Puritanism of the Commonwealth",origin:"George Fox, a shoemaker's apprentice who found no comfort in any preacher, concluded that Christ could speak to his condition directly, and in 1652, standing on Pendle Hill, saw a great people to be gathered. He gathered them in the north of England within a few years: seekers, ex-Baptists and disillusioned soldiers who accepted that the light of Christ is in every person and that no steeple-house, hireling minister or outward sacrament is required. The movement was persecuted with unusual severity — thousands imprisoned under the Quaker Act of 1662 and the Conventicle Acts, four hanged on Boston Common between 1659 and 1661, among them Mary Dyer — partly because Friends refused the oath, refused hat-honour to social superiors, and would not stop meeting. William Penn's Pennsylvania, chartered in 1681, was their experiment in governing without those weapons.",distinctives:["That of God in everyone, and worship as expectant waiting in silence in which anyone present may be moved to speak — the unprogrammed meeting, without clergy, liturgy, baptism or communion, since all life is sacramental.","The peace testimony, declared to Charles II in January 1661 and never withdrawn, and a corresponding tradition of conscientious objection, relief work and mediation; the Friends Service Council and the American Friends Service Committee shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 1947.","Equality expressed as practice: plain speech, refusal of titles and of oaths, and women in ministry from the beginning — Margaret Fell's Women's Speaking Justified of 1666 is one of the earliest defences of it in English.","Abolition: the Germantown Protest of 1688 is the first known antislavery document in colonial America; John Woolman persuaded Friends to free their own slaves through the 1750s and 1760s, and Quakers were disproportionately represented in British abolition thereafter.","Serious internal divisions in the nineteenth century — Hicksite and Orthodox in 1827, then Gurneyite and Wilburite — and the emergence of a pastoral, programmed, evangelical Quakerism with paid ministers, hymns and sermons, which is now the majority form.","Most Friends today are neither silent nor Western: the Friends Church in Kenya is the largest body of Quakers in the world, and there are substantial evangelical Friends churches in Bolivia, Guatemala and Burundi."],christology:"Historically Christ-centred and, in the early period, deliberately unsystematic: Fox spoke of Christ come to teach his people himself, and of the inward light as the light of Christ rather than a natural human faculty. Evangelical Friends today hold a straightforwardly orthodox Christology and a written statement of faith; liberal unprogrammed meetings, particularly in Britain and the American north-east, are theologically diverse and include members who are not Christian in any confessional sense. The Society contains both and has largely stopped trying to adjudicate between them.",today:"Around 370,000 to 400,000 Friends worldwide, well over half of them in Africa. British Quakers number about 13,000 and American Friends around 75,000, divided among several bodies that are not in full fellowship with one another."},{slug:"methodists",name:"The Methodists",selfName:"the People called Methodists; Wesleyan",exonym:"Methodists — a jibe at the Oxford Holy Club's methodical devotions",era:"1738 – present",group:"the-english-speaking-explosion",parent:"The Church of England",origin:"John Wesley, an Anglican priest and fellow of Lincoln College, did not intend to leave the Church of England and never formally did. What he intended was a society within it for people seeking holiness. After a failed mission to Georgia and decisive conversations with Moravians, he found assurance at a meeting in Aldersgate Street on 24 May 1738 and began, reluctantly, to preach in fields to people the parish system was not reaching — colliers at Kingswood, tinners in Cornwall, the new industrial poor. His brother Charles wrote the hymns, some six thousand of them, and the hymns carried the theology. Wesley organised converts into classes and bands with weekly examination, licensed lay preachers, and rode an estimated quarter of a million miles. In 1784 he ordained ministers for America himself, having no bishop willing to do it, and thereby made separation inevitable; the Christmas Conference in Baltimore that year constituted the Methodist Episcopal Church.",distinctives:["Arminian rather than Calvinist: Christ died for all, prevenient grace goes before conversion, and the offer is genuinely universal. This split Methodism early — George Whitefield's Calvinistic Methodism became the Presbyterian Church of Wales.","Christian perfection, or entire sanctification: the claim that a believer may in this life be so filled with love as to be free from wilful sin. It is Wesley's most distinctive and most contested doctrine, and the seed of the later Holiness and Pentecostal movements.","Assurance — the witness of the Spirit that one is a child of God — preached as available to ordinary people, and the connexional organisation of classes, circuits and conferences that made a movement out of the experience.","Hymnody as catechesis: Charles Wesley's texts taught the doctrine of the atonement to people who could not read the Articles.","The African Methodist Episcopal Church, founded by Richard Allen in 1816 after he and others were pulled from their knees at St George's in Philadelphia in an incident traditionally dated to 1787, is the oldest independent Black denomination in the United States and the institutional root of much of the Black Church tradition, alongside the AME Zion and CME churches.","American Methodism divided over slavery in 1844 and reunited in 1939 on terms that segregated Black congregations into a separate jurisdiction until 1968. Between 2019 and 2024 roughly a quarter of United Methodist congregations in the United States disaffiliated over sexuality, most forming or joining the Global Methodist Church."],christology:"Chalcedonian, received from Anglicanism and expressed chiefly in hymns rather than in confessions; Wesley's theological originality lies in grace and sanctification rather than in the doctrine of Christ's person.",today:"The World Methodist Council claims about 80 million adherents across some 138 countries. The United Methodist Church, still the largest single body, reports around nine million after the disaffiliations; Methodism's most vigorous churches are now in Nigeria, Ghana, Kenya, the Democratic Republic of Congo and Korea, and the Korean Methodist Church alone numbers well over a million."},{slug:"stone-campbell",name:"The Stone-Campbell Restoration Movement",selfName:"Christians only; Disciples of Christ; Churches of Christ",exonym:"Campbellites",era:"1801 – present",group:"the-restorationists",parent:"American Presbyterianism on the frontier",origin:"Two Presbyterian secessions met and merged. Barton W. Stone presided over the Cane Ridge camp meeting in Kentucky in August 1801, an event of such scale and emotional intensity that it became the template for the Second Great Awakening, and in 1804 dissolved his presbytery in a document called the Last Will and Testament of the Springfield Presbytery, resolving that his followers should be called Christians and nothing else. Thomas Campbell, an Irish Seceder minister in Pennsylvania, published the Declaration and Address in 1809 arguing that the church of Christ is essentially one and that human creeds are the cause of division; his son Alexander built the movement's press, colleges and arguments. Stone's Christians and Campbell's Disciples united at Lexington in 1832. The aim was not another denomination but the end of denominations.",distinctives:["Where the scriptures speak, we speak; where the scriptures are silent, we are silent — a hermeneutic of command, example and necessary inference, applied to church practice with considerable rigour.","Baptism by immersion for the remission of sins, weekly communion presided over by lay elders, congregational autonomy, and a refusal of denominational names and of binding creeds.","Christians only, but not the only Christians: the movement's founding intention was unity, and its founding irony is that it produced three separate bodies.","The a cappella question — whether instrumental music in worship is authorised by the New Testament — together with the missionary society question, divided the movement; the 1906 United States religious census listed Churches of Christ separately for the first time, formalising a split already complete.","Three streams today: the Churches of Christ (a cappella, non-institutional in part), the independent Christian Churches and Churches of Christ, and the Christian Church (Disciples of Christ), which restructured as a denomination in 1968 and is ecumenically engaged and theologically liberal.","The movement produced the first significant American argument that the New Testament pattern could simply be re-enacted, an idea that has recurred in a hundred later groups."],christology:"Orthodox and Chalcedonian in substance, though the movement has generally declined to use creedal language on principle: it confesses that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of the living God, and treats the demand for anything more precise as the beginning of division. Alexander Campbell contended in print against Unitarian readings and defended Christ's full divinity while refusing the term Trinity as unscriptural vocabulary.",today:"Churches of Christ number perhaps 1.1 to 1.5 million in the United States and have been declining for two decades; the independent Christian Churches around a million; the Disciples of Christ under 250,000 and falling steeply from a mid-century peak of nearly two million. Outside the United States the movement is strongest in India, Nigeria, Zimbabwe, Brazil and the Philippines, where its total membership may now exceed the American."},{slug:"seventh-day-adventists",name:"The Seventh-day Adventists",selfName:"Seventh-day Adventist Church",exonym:null,era:"1863 – present",group:"the-restorationists",parent:"The Millerite Adventist movement",origin:"William Miller, a Baptist lay preacher, calculated from Daniel 8:14 that Christ would return in 1843 or 1844, and perhaps fifty thousand Americans believed him. When 22 October 1844 passed, the movement collapsed in what its survivors called the Great Disappointment. A small group concluded that the date had been right and the event misidentified: what began in 1844 was not Christ's return to earth but his entry into the heavenly sanctuary's Most Holy Place to begin a work of investigative judgement. To this Joseph Bates added the seventh-day Sabbath, and Ellen G. White — who from 1844 until her death in 1915 reported some two thousand visions and prophetic dreams — added the pastoral and organisational leadership that turned a disappointed remnant into a church, formally organised at Battle Creek in 1863.",distinctives:["The seventh-day Sabbath, from Friday sunset to Saturday sunset, kept as a creation ordinance and, in Adventist eschatology, as the eventual test of allegiance.","The sanctuary doctrine and the investigative judgement, unique to Adventism and its most disputed teaching internally; the 1980 Glacier View review of Desmond Ford's critique of it was the church's most serious modern doctrinal crisis.","Conditional immortality: the dead sleep until the resurrection, and the finally impenitent are destroyed rather than eternally tormented — a position Adventists share with a few other bodies and hold against most of the tradition.","Ellen White's writings are held to be inspired counsel, a continuing manifestation of the prophetic gift, while scripture remains the sole standard of doctrine; her own image for this was a lesser light leading to the greater light.","A health message — vegetarianism, abstinence from alcohol and tobacco, and a theology of the body — that produced the Battle Creek Sanitarium, the breakfast cereal industry by way of J. H. Kellogg, and Loma Linda University; Adventists in California have been studied for decades as one of the world's longest-lived populations.","One of the largest Protestant education and health networks in existence: some nine thousand schools and colleges and over two hundred hospitals and sanatoriums with several hundred clinics, and a strong historic commitment to religious liberty for everyone, not only for themselves."],christology:"Trinitarian and Chalcedonian, and explicitly so in the church's Fundamental Beliefs — though this was not always so: several founders, including James White and Joseph Bates, began with anti-trinitarian and semi-Arian views inherited from the Christian Connexion, and the church moved to a settled trinitarianism over the first half of the twentieth century. Most evangelicals have regarded Adventism as an orthodox if idiosyncratic Protestant body since the exchanges surrounding Questions on Doctrine in 1957; a minority still dissent.",today:"About 22 to 23 million baptised members worldwide, growing steadily, with well over half in Africa and Latin America — Rwanda, Zambia, Brazil, the Philippines and Kenya hold some of the largest fields, and North America is now a small minority of the church. Membership figures are of baptised adults and are audited, though the church acknowledges significant lapse rates."},{slug:"latter-day-saints",name:"The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints",selfName:"The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints; Latter-day Saints; Saints",exonym:"Mormons",era:"1830 – present",group:"the-restorationists",parent:"The religious ferment of upstate New York; the church's own account is direct restoration by revelation",origin:"Joseph Smith Jr, a farm boy in western New York, reported that in about 1820 he asked God which church to join and was answered by a visitation of the Father and the Son, and that an angel later directed him to golden plates from which he translated the Book of Mormon, published in 1830. He organised the Church of Christ on 6 April 1830 with six members. The church's own account is that this was not reform but restoration: the authority of the apostles had been lost in an ancient apostasy and was restored to Smith by heavenly messengers. Persecution and expulsion followed from New York to Ohio, Missouri — where the governor issued an order that the Saints be expelled or exterminated in 1838 — and Illinois, where Smith was killed by a mob at Carthage on 27 June 1844. Brigham Young led the main body across the plains to the Salt Lake Valley in 1847, and the church built a commonwealth in the Great Basin.",distinctives:["Continuing revelation through a living prophet and twelve apostles, and an open canon: the Bible, the Book of Mormon, the Doctrine and Covenants and the Pearl of Great Price.","Temple ordinances distinct from Sunday worship — endowment, marriage sealed for eternity, and baptism performed on behalf of the dead — which underlie the church's vast genealogical archive, the largest in the world and freely available.","A lay ministry with no paid local clergy, congregations led by members called from within, and a missionary force of tens of thousands of young men and women serving at their own expense; alongside it the Word of Wisdom's abstinence from alcohol, tobacco, tea and coffee, a strong tithing discipline, and a welfare and disaster-relief system of considerable scale.","Plural marriage was practised privately from the 1840s, publicly taught from 1852, and formally ended by the Manifesto of 1890 and a second in 1904; the church now excommunicates those who practise it, and the fundamentalist groups that continue it are separate bodies with no connection to it.","A priesthood restriction barring members of Black African descent from ordination and temple ordinances was in force from the mid-nineteenth century until a revelation ended it in 1978; in 2013 the church published an essay disavowing the theories once advanced to explain it and condemning racism.","The church asks to be called by its full name and, since 2018, has requested that Mormon and LDS not be used as substitutes for it. This portal honours that request and notes the exonym only because readers will arrive searching for it."],christology:"In their own words: Jesus Christ is the literal Son of God, the Jehovah of the Old Testament, the Creator under the Father, the sinless Redeemer whose atonement in Gethsemane and on the cross saves all who come to him, and the resurrected and living head of the church. Latter-day Saints therefore describe themselves as Christians, and the name of Christ is in the name of the church. Where they part from the councils is on the nature of the Godhead: they hold the Father, the Son and the Holy Ghost to be three distinct beings, one in purpose rather than one in substance, with the Father and the Son possessing glorified bodies, and they reject ὁμοούσιος and the creeds that formulated it as post-apostolic. They further teach that human beings are the spirit offspring of God and may, through Christ, become like him — the doctrine of exaltation. For these reasons trinitarian churches classify the Latter-day Saints as outside the Nicene confession and generally do not recognise their baptism; Latter-day Saints reply that the Nicene confession was itself the departure. Both statements should be read as what each side actually holds.",today:"About 17.5 million members of record worldwide, of whom rather more than half live outside the United States, with the largest concentrations in Mexico, Brazil and the Philippines; the church counts members of record rather than participants, and independent estimates of active participation are considerably lower. The Community of Christ, formerly the Reorganized Church, is a separate body of around a quarter of a million with a very different trajectory, having moved toward mainline Protestant theology and practice."},{slug:"jehovahs-witnesses",name:"Jehovah's Witnesses",selfName:"Jehovah's Witnesses",exonym:"Russellites; Millennial Dawnists",era:"1879 – present",group:"the-restorationists",parent:"The Adventist and Bible Student milieu of nineteenth-century America",origin:"Charles Taze Russell, a Pittsburgh haberdasher who had lost his faith in hellfire and recovered a faith in the Bible through contact with Adventists, began publishing Zion's Watch Tower in 1879 and organised Bible Student congregations around it. He taught that Christ had returned invisibly and that a divine kingdom was at hand. After his death in 1916 the movement was consolidated, and substantially reshaped, by Joseph Franklin Rutherford, who centralised authority, launched door-to-door preaching as the obligation of every member, and in 1931 adopted the name Jehovah's Witnesses from Isaiah 43:10. The organisation is directed from its world headquarters by a Governing Body.",distinctives:["The divine name: God is Jehovah, and the restoration of the name to worship and to their own translation of the Bible is a defining commitment — carried out through door-to-door and public witnessing understood as the responsibility of every baptised member. 'Publisher' is their ordinary word for a member.","Political neutrality: no military service, no voting, no saluting flags or national anthems, no participation in interfaith activity. This has cost them heavily and has also produced landmark law — the United States Supreme Court reversed itself on the compulsory flag salute in West Virginia v. Barnette in 1943 after Witness children were expelled from schools.","Refusal of blood transfusions, understood from the apostolic decree of Acts 15, together with a developed system of bloodless surgical alternatives that has been influential in medicine well beyond the movement.","An eschatology of two hopes: 144,000 anointed who reign with Christ in heaven, and a great crowd who will live for ever on a restored paradise earth; no immortal soul, no hellfire, and destruction rather than eternal torment for the wicked. Dates have been a recurring difficulty: Russell's movement expected the visible consummation of the age in 1914 and, when that did not occur, retained the date with a changed meaning — Witnesses now hold that 1914 marks the beginning of Christ's invisible heavenly kingship. Later expectations gathered around 1925 and, less officially, 1975; the movement's own literature has since acknowledged those expectations were mistaken and that its publications had encouraged them.","Persecution: around 1,500 Witnesses died in Nazi camps, where they wore the purple triangle and were, unusually, offered release in exchange for a signed renunciation that most refused. The Russian Federation banned the organisation as extremist in 2017 and has imprisoned members since.","Serious external criticism, which an honest entry must record: the practice of disfellowshipping and the shunning of former members, including by their own families, and the handling of child sexual abuse allegations, examined critically by the Australian Royal Commission in 2015 and by the Charity Commission for England and Wales."],christology:"In their own terms: Jesus is God's only-begotten Son, the first of Jehovah's creations, the one through whom all other things were made, identified with the archangel Michael, who gave his perfect human life as a ransom and now rules as king of God's kingdom. He is to be honoured but not worshipped as Almighty God, and the holy spirit is God's active force rather than a person. The Witnesses reject the Trinity explicitly as a post-biblical accretion. Trinitarian churches identify this as a form of the subordinationism condemned at Nicaea in 325; the Witnesses reject the historical label, holding that they derive their position from scripture alone and not from Arius, whom they do not cite as an authority. Both descriptions are recorded here, as each side gives it.",today:"About 8.8 million active publishers reporting in more than 230 lands, with attendance at the annual Memorial of Christ's death exceeding 20 million — the gap between the two figures is itself informative about the movement's reach and its standards of membership. Growth has slowed in the West and continues in parts of Africa and Latin America."},{slug:"pentecostalism",name:"Pentecostalism and the Charismatic Renewal",selfName:"Full Gospel; Apostolic Faith; Spirit-filled; renewalists",exonym:"Holy Rollers",era:"1901 – present",group:"the-global-south",parent:"The Wesleyan Holiness movement, the healing revivals, and African American worship",origin:"On 1 January 1901 a student at Charles Parham's Bible school in Topeka, Agnes Ozman, spoke in tongues, and Parham concluded that this was the biblical evidence of baptism in the Holy Spirit. The movement was made not there but in Los Angeles. William J. Seymour, a one-eyed Black holiness preacher and the son of former slaves, had received Parham's teaching in Houston while segregation obliged him to listen from the corridor outside the classroom. In April 1906 he began meetings that moved to a former African Methodist Episcopal building at 312 Azusa Street, and for three years they ran daily and continuously, without a printed order, drawing Black, white, Latino and Asian worshippers together in a city where that did not happen. Frank Bartleman, who was there, wrote that the colour line was washed away in the blood. Visitors carried the fire home; within two years the revival had reached Norway, India, Chile and West Africa.",distinctives:["Baptism in the Holy Spirit as a distinct experience subsequent to conversion, with speaking in tongues as its initial evidence in classical Pentecostal teaching — a claim later charismatics often hold more loosely.","The restoration of all the spiritual gifts named by Paul: prophecy, healing, discernment, interpretation and tongues, exercised by ordinary members rather than by clergy.","Oral, participatory, musical worship with testimony and expectancy at its centre, which made the movement portable into cultures the printed European confessions could not reach — and which is much of the explanation for its scale.","Interracial at its origin and re-segregated within a decade, as white ministers withdrew into separate bodies; the Assemblies of God formed in 1914 and the Church of God in Christ under Charles Harrison Mason became the largest Black Pentecostal denomination in America. The 'Memphis Miracle' of 1994 was an attempt at reconciliation between white and Black Pentecostal bodies.","The Charismatic Renewal carried the same experience into the historic churches without leaving them: Dennis Bennett announced his Spirit baptism to his Episcopal parish in Van Nuys in 1960, and the Catholic Charismatic Renewal began with a student retreat at Duquesne University in February 1967, later encouraged by Paul VI and successive popes. A 'third wave' of independent and Vineyard-style churches followed from the 1980s.","The prosperity gospel, which teaches that faith produces material wealth and health, has spread widely with Pentecostalism and is vigorously criticised from inside it; it is a movement within the movement, not the movement itself, and Pentecostal theologians have been among its sharpest critics."],christology:"Chalcedonian and trinitarian in the great majority of Pentecostal bodies, organised around a fourfold or fivefold gospel: Jesus as saviour, sanctifier, healer, baptiser in the Spirit and coming king. Pentecostal theology is Christ-centred rather than Spirit-centred in its own self-understanding — the Spirit is given to make Jesus present.",today:"The largest single development in modern Christianity and the hardest to count. Pew's 2011 study estimated some 279 million Pentecostals and 305 million charismatics, about a quarter of all Christians; the Center for the Study of Global Christianity has put the combined figure above 600 million. These counts overlap heavily with Catholic, Anglican and independent membership, depend on self-identification, and should be treated as orders of magnitude rather than measurements. Brazil, Nigeria, the Philippines, Korea, Kenya and Guatemala are among the movement's principal centres."},{slug:"oneness-pentecostals",name:"Oneness Pentecostalism",selfName:"Apostolic; Oneness; Jesus' Name believers",exonym:"Jesus Only; modalists",era:"1913 – present",group:"the-global-south",parent:"Early Pentecostalism",origin:"At a camp meeting at Arroyo Seco, near Los Angeles, in 1913, a Canadian preacher named R. E. McAlister observed in a baptismal sermon that the apostles in Acts baptised in the name of Jesus rather than with the trinitarian formula of Matthew 28. The remark set off a year of study and prayer, and in April 1914 Frank Ewart and Glenn Cook rebaptised each other in the name of Jesus Christ. What Pentecostals called the New Issue spread rapidly and split the young movement: the Assemblies of God adopted its Statement of Fundamental Truths in 1916 specifically to exclude the teaching, and about a quarter of its ministers left. The Pentecostal Assemblies of the World, which remained interracial far longer than most American bodies, and later the United Pentecostal Church International, formed in 1945, became the principal organisations.",distinctives:["Baptism performed in the name of Jesus Christ, following the pattern of Acts 2:38 and 8:16 rather than the formula of Matthew 28:19, which Oneness believers read as naming the one name rather than prescribing three words.","The new birth understood as a threefold apostolic pattern: repentance, baptism in Jesus' name, and the baptism of the Holy Spirit evidenced by speaking in tongues.","A strong tradition of practical holiness in dress, entertainment and conduct in many Oneness bodies, though this varies widely and has relaxed in some.","They object to the label 'Jesus Only', which suggests a denial of the Father, and many object to 'modalist' as well: their theologians distinguish their position from ancient Sabellianism, holding that the Father is the eternal Spirit of God, the Son is the authentic and complete humanity in which God was manifest, and that this is a distinction of manifestation rather than a succession of masks. Trinitarian theologians reply that whatever the intent, the position denies eternal distinctions in God and so falls under the ancient condemnation of monarchian modalism. The disagreement is genuine and neither side should be paraphrased by the other.","Oneness Pentecostalism is global and often indigenous: the Apostolic Church of the Faith in Christ Jesus in Mexico, the Iglesia Pentecostal Unida in Colombia, large Nigerian and Ethiopian bodies, and the True Jesus Church of Chinese origin all hold a Oneness position independently of the American organisations."],christology:"The heart of the tradition. There is one God, indivisible, who is Spirit; Jesus Christ is that one God manifest in flesh, so that the fullness of the Godhead dwells bodily in him — Father in creation, Son in redemption, Spirit in the church, one person and not three. Every title of deity in scripture is therefore his. Trinitarian churches judge this incompatible with the Nicene confession of three co-eternal persons; Oneness believers answer that they confess the full and undiminished deity of Jesus Christ more strongly than their critics do, and that it is the doctrine of three persons that compromises monotheism. Both positions are stated here as their holders state them.",today:"Perhaps 20 to 25 million Oneness Pentecostals worldwide by scholarly estimate, the largest single organisation being the United Pentecostal Church International with several million adherents across more than 200 nations. Relations with trinitarian Pentecostal bodies remain formally broken, and Oneness churches are excluded from most evangelical and Pentecostal fellowships on doctrinal grounds."},{slug:"african-instituted-churches",name:"The African Instituted Churches",selfName:"African Independent, Initiated or Instituted Churches; Aladura; Zionist; Kimbanguist",exonym:"separatist churches; syncretists",era:"1892 – present",group:"the-global-south",parent:"Mission Christianity in Africa, refounded on African terms",origin:"From the late nineteenth century African Christians began founding churches of their own, for two reasons that ran together: mission churches would not give Africans leadership, and mission Christianity had no answer to the questions Africans actually brought to it — sickness, witchcraft, barrenness, dreams and the ancestors. The Ethiopian churches came first, secessions over African leadership rather than doctrine, beginning with Mangena Mokone's Ethiopian Church in South Africa in 1892 and taking their name from the psalm's promise that Ethiopia shall stretch out her hands to God. The prophetic and Zionist churches followed, with healing and the Spirit at the centre. In West Africa the Aladura — the Yoruba word means owners of prayer — arose during the influenza pandemic of 1918. In the Congo, Simon Kimbangu, a Baptist catechist, began healing at Nkamba on 6 April 1921; the Belgian authorities arrested him within five months, commuted a death sentence to life imprisonment, and he died in prison thirty years later in 1951, by which time a church had formed around his name.",distinctives:["Healing, prophecy, dreams and deliverance from spiritual powers as the ordinary business of church life — an engagement with the African spiritual world that mission Christianity had refused to conduct.","African leadership, African languages, African music and drumming, white robes and bare feet, prohibitions on alcohol, tobacco and sometimes pork, and holy places of pilgrimage: Nkamba for Kimbanguists, Moria in Limpopo for the Zion Christian Church, whose Easter gathering is among the largest annual religious assemblies in Africa.","Named traditions of great size: the Zion Christian Church founded by Engenas Lekganyane in the 1920s; the Nazareth Baptist Church of Isaiah Shembe from 1910; Christ Apostolic Church out of Joseph Ayo Babalola's Oke-Oye revival of 1930; the Cherubim and Seraphim of Moses Orimolade and Christianah Abiodun Akinsowon; the Church of the Lord (Aladura) of Josiah Oshitelu; the Celestial Church of Christ founded by Samuel Oschoffa at Porto-Novo in 1947.","Political weight without political programme: Kimbangu's movement was treated as sedition by the Belgian administration precisely because a church Africans ran was, in a colony, a political fact.","The Kimbanguist Church was the first African Instituted Church admitted to the World Council of Churches, in 1969. Some later Kimbanguist teaching — concerning the status of Simon Kimbangu in relation to the Holy Spirit, and the role of his three sons — has been read by other churches as departing from trinitarian orthodoxy, and has caused both internal division and ecumenical difficulty; the church's own leadership disputes those readings. The matter is unresolved and is reported here as contested rather than settled.","Accusations of syncretism have followed these churches from the start, and African theologians have replied that what is called syncretism in Africa is called inculturation when European Christianity does it."],christology:"Overwhelmingly orthodox in intention and confession: Christ as saviour, and above all as the victor over the powers — a Christus Victor emphasis that answers the questions the culture asks. Prophetic founders are honoured in some of these churches in ways that other Christians find hard to distinguish from the honour due to Christ, and the churches themselves generally deny the equation. The Kimbanguist case above is the most-discussed instance.",today:"Collectively very large and very hard to count: estimates of AIC membership across Africa range from 60 million to over 100 million, depending entirely on which independent and Pentecostal churches are included in the category. The Zion Christian Church is probably the largest single body in southern Africa, and the Kimbanguist Church claims several million in the Democratic Republic of Congo, Angola and the Republic of Congo. Most of these churches now have diaspora congregations in Europe and North America."},{slug:"chinese-house-churches",name:"The Chinese Independent Churches",selfName:"家庭教会 (jiating jiaohui, house churches); 真耶穌教會 (the True Jesus Church); the Little Flock",exonym:"underground church",era:"1917 – present",group:"the-global-south",parent:"Protestant missions in China, and their rejection",origin:"Christianity reached China three times before it stayed: the Church of the East in 635, the Jesuits under Matteo Ricci from 1583, and Protestant missions from Robert Morrison in 1807. What made it Chinese was the independent church movement of the 1910s and 1920s, when Chinese Christians founded bodies with no foreign money and no foreign control. The True Jesus Church was established in Beijing in 1917 by Wei Enbo, and is Oneness, Sabbatarian and Pentecostal. Watchman Nee's Little Flock, from 1922, built an assembly movement of great influence, and Jing Dianying's Jesus Family formed rural communes. After 1949 the new state organised Protestants into the Three-Self Patriotic Movement and imprisoned those who would not join: Wang Mingdao from 1955, Watchman Nee until his death in prison in 1972. The Cultural Revolution closed every church in China. When they reopened after 1979, the church that emerged was larger than the one that had been suppressed.",distinctives:["A dual structure that has lasted forty years: registered congregations under the Three-Self Patriotic Movement and the China Christian Council, and unregistered house churches and networks that decline registration on the ground that Christ, not the state, governs the church.","The True Jesus Church as a fully indigenous Chinese denomination with its own distinctive doctrine — baptism by face-down immersion in living water in the name of Jesus, footwashing, the seventh-day Sabbath, and unison prayer in tongues — now international, with its largest open presence in Taiwan.","Watchman Nee and Witness Lee's legacy in the Local Churches movement and Living Stream Ministry, and Nee's devotional writings, which have circulated worldwide far beyond any Chinese context.","Rural networks of great size built by lay evangelists through the 1980s and 1990s, and since 2000 a substantial urban, professional and intellectual Christianity in the cities, of which Early Rain Covenant Church in Chengdu was the most public example until its suppression in 2018 and the imprisonment of its pastor Wang Yi in 2019.","Catholics face the same division between the state-supervised Catholic Patriotic Association and the underground church loyal to Rome; the Holy See and Beijing signed a provisional agreement on the appointment of bishops in 2018, renewed since, which Catholics themselves assess very differently.","Since 2018 a tightened regulatory regime and a policy of sinicisation have closed churches, removed crosses, restricted minors from attending and moved worship online and back into homes."],christology:"Predominantly evangelical and trinitarian, shaped by conservative Protestant missions and by the independent movements' own reading of scripture. The True Jesus Church is a significant exception, holding a Oneness position independently arrived at, and it is generally not in fellowship with trinitarian Chinese churches.",today:"Nobody knows the number, and anyone who gives one without a range should be distrusted. Estimates of Protestants in China run from roughly 20 million to close to 100 million: the state-registered churches report around 38 million; a Pew analysis published in 2023 found no evidence for continued rapid growth and put Christian identification at a low single-digit percentage of adults; some researchers and mission agencies maintain far higher figures based on house-church network claims. Catholics number perhaps 10 to 12 million across both structures. What is not in doubt is that Chinese Christianity is now overwhelmingly led and funded by Chinese Christians."},{slug:"iglesia-ni-cristo",name:"Iglesia ni Cristo",selfName:"Iglesia ni Cristo — the Church of Christ",exonym:"Manalistas",era:"1914 – present",group:"the-global-south",parent:"Its own account: the restored first-century Church of Christ, not a branch of any other body",origin:"Felix Y. Manalo, a Filipino who had passed through Catholicism, Methodism, Presbyterianism and Adventism, registered Iglesia ni Cristo with the civil authorities in Manila on 27 July 1914. The church does not regard that date as its founding: its teaching is that Christ founded one church in the first century, that it fell away into apostasy after the apostles, and that it was re-established in the last days in the Philippines, with Manalo as God's last messenger — the sugo — sent to gather the people identified in Isaiah as coming from the far east and in Revelation as sealed by the angel ascending from the rising of the sun. It grew from a handful of members in Punta, Santa Ana into the largest indigenous Christian body in Asia, and it is not a Protestant church, a Catholic church or a Pentecostal one; it stands on its own account outside all three.",distinctives:["One church as the vehicle of salvation: membership of the Iglesia is understood as necessary, and its unity is maintained by a centralised ministry under an Executive Minister — Felix Manalo to 1963, Eraño Manalo to 2009, and Eduardo V. Manalo since.","Worship twice weekly with attendance recorded, seating divided by sex, congregational singing from the church's own hymnal, no images or statues of any kind, and no observance of Christmas or Easter as such.","Bloc voting: the membership votes as a body in Philippine elections on the direction of the church's leadership, which has made it a considerable political factor.","A distinctive architecture — steeply pitched roofs, banks of vertical spires, and a nationwide building programme — and the Philippine Arena, opened for the centennial in 2014 with a capacity around 55,000, the largest indoor arena in the world.","Extensive charitable, educational and disaster-relief work through its Felix Y. Manalo Foundation and Lingap sa Mamamayan programme, and congregations established in more than a hundred countries following Filipino migration.","The Philippine Independent Church, or Aglipayan Church, is a separate and earlier Filipino body, founded in 1902 in the aftermath of the revolution against Spain, episcopal in order and now in full communion with the Anglican Communion — a different answer to a related question about a Filipino church."],christology:"Iglesia ni Cristo teaches that there is one God, the Father, and that Jesus Christ is a man — sinless, born of the virgin, the Son of God by God's own declaration, the mediator, the saviour who died for humanity and was raised, and now the head of the church, but a man and not God. The Holy Spirit is understood as the power of God rather than a person. The church rejects the Trinity as a doctrine formulated by the councils rather than taught by Christ, and holds that this is the apostolic faith restored. Trinitarian churches accordingly regard it as outside the Nicene confession. This portal records the church's own statement of what it believes and the historical comparison side by side, without collapsing either into the other.",today:"The 2020 Philippine census recorded roughly 2.8 million adherents in the Philippines, about 2.6 per cent of the population, making it the largest church in the country after the Catholic Church. The church itself does not publish membership figures and reports congregations in over 150 countries and territories, chiefly among the Filipino diaspora. It remains one of the very few large Christian bodies founded in Asia and led entirely from Asia."}]};export{e as default};
